/* CCAR-P — Claude Certified Architect, Professional.
   The "Go deeper" explanation under each lesson: the long-form prose its body,
   points and traps summarise — the mechanism, how the points connect, the
   decision rule and a worked example. Written from the CCAR-P study site and study guide.

   Keyed by lesson ref. Each entry is a list of blocks: a string is a
   paragraph, { h } a subheading, { example, label? } a worked example and
   { code, label? } a code block. Text may carry <b>, <i> and <code>, as in the
   lesson files. data.js attaches each entry to its lesson as `more`; loaded
   before data.js. */

window.CCARP_DEEP = {

  "1.1": [
    { h: "Discovery produces three artefacts" },
    "A Professional scenario rarely hands you a clean requirement. It hands you a sponsor's phrase — \"we want an agent\", \"add AI to the claims process\" — followed by a paragraph of facts, and the facts decide the answer. Treat the phrase as a hypothesis that discovery will confirm or reject, not as a design brief.",
    "Discovery is finished when three things are written down: the problem in business terms, measurable success criteria, and the constraint list. Until those exist, an architecture is a guess and a proof of concept tests the wrong thing. A PoC can come earlier only as a deliberate feasibility probe with its own exit criteria; it is never the default first step. This is why sequencing items so often have the least technical option as the right one: choosing a framework, load-testing an endpoint and pinning a model ID are all real work, but they come after the design exists.",
    { h: "Ask \"does this need a model?\" of every step" },
    "The model-or-not question is best asked of each step, not of the whole system. Most enterprise processes are a mixture. Reading a customer's free-text complaint is a language problem; checking whether the purchase date falls inside the return window is a date comparison. The design that wins puts Claude on the unstructured, judgement-heavy steps and leaves rules, lookups, arithmetic and threshold checks in code, where they are deterministic, cheap, fast and auditable. Putting a model on a deterministic step adds cost, latency and a non-zero error rate to something that previously had none.",
    "The opposite mistake exists too. A rule engine asked to cope with hundreds of phrasings of the same complaint, or documents whose layout changes every week, becomes an unmaintainable pile of special cases. That long-tail variety is exactly where a language model earns its place, which is why the right answer is usually a hybrid rather than \"all model\" or \"no model\".",
    { h: "Shape and constraints do most of the design work" },
    "Naming the capability shape tells you the pattern, the metric and the risk at once. Classification suggests a cheap tier and a confusion matrix; extraction suggests a schema, field-level accuracy and explicit handling of missing values; summarisation is measured on faithfulness; generation on a rubric; conversation on multi-turn behaviour and escalation. Acting is the shape that changes the risk profile most, because actions have consequences and reversibility becomes the first question. Many requests combine several shapes, which is the cue to decompose them (see 1.5).",
    "Constraints are the facts that eliminate options, so read the stem for them before reading the answers. A residency requirement can force a particular cloud route (see 2.1). A tight latency budget rules out long chains and heavy reasoning. A per-transaction cost ceiling rules out the top tier on every call. An irreversible action forces a gate. A named person who must sign off turns full automation into automated drafting with human approval — it constrains the output stage, not whether the model drafts at all.",
    { example: "An insurer asks for \"a generative AI solution to speed up claims intake\", targeting a 40% cut in intake time. Discovery finds that 70% of claims arrive through a web form whose fields are already typed, 30% arrive as emailed letters with photos, and coverage decisions must stay with adjusters. One option is an agent that processes every claim end to end; another runs Claude extraction on the emailed claims only, sends web-form data straight to the existing rules engine, and leaves the decision in the adjuster's queue; a third has Claude re-read every web-form claim to double-check the typed fields; a fourth starts a PoC next week to see what the model can do. The second is right: the model works only where the input is unstructured, and the decision constraint is respected. The end-to-end agent is the tempting answer because it seems to cover everything, but it routes already-structured data through a model for no gain and edges towards a decision the constraint reserves for people." }
  ],

  "1.2": [
    { h: "Four stages, each with a job" },
    "The four stages are not a diagram convention; each one owns a distinct kind of failure. <b>Ingestion</b> owns what enters the system: validating and normalising inputs, redacting personal data before it reaches the model, and recording where each item came from. <b>Processing</b> owns the model calls, retrieval, tools and orchestration. <b>Output</b> owns what leaves: schema validation, business rules checked in code, delivery, and a human gate where the stakes require one. <b>Feedback</b> owns whether the system is still good next quarter.",
    "The output stage is where \"the model returned an answer\" stops being the end of the story. Valid structure is not valid content: a schema guarantees that a total is a number, never that it is the right number. Anything consequential is checked against a source, a deterministic calculation or a person before it is acted on.",
    { h: "The feedback loop is how quality survives" },
    "A system without a feedback loop does not fail loudly; it decays. Inputs drift — new product names, new document layouts, new kinds of request — while the prompt, retrieval and model stay exactly as they were on launch day. The signature the exam uses is precise: quality falls over months, nothing in the code or prompts changed, the model ID is pinned, and latency and error rates are flat. Error-rate monitoring cannot see this, because a misclassified claim is a perfectly successful HTTP call.",
    "A real loop has three parts. It captures signals — user ratings, corrections, overrides, escalations, items a human had to fix. It routes each failure to an owner. And it turns failures into evaluation cases that drive prompt, retrieval and dataset updates. A stronger model, a second verification pass or prompt caching are the common distractors here: none of them detects drift, and \"upgrade the model\" without a diagnosis is guesswork.",
    { h: "Design the unhappy path explicitly" },
    "Enumerate the ways a request can fail — low confidence, retrieval miss, tool error, policy refusal, timeout — and give each a trigger, a route and an owner. The trigger is a threshold or a classifier; the route is retry, fallback, escalate or decline; the owner is a named queue with a turnaround expectation. Those are architectural decisions. The wording of an uncertainty disclaimer or the retry sampling settings are implementation details that can wait.",
    "Separation of concerns is what makes all of this workable. When prompt logic, tools, retrieval, validation and orchestration are separate modules, you can evaluate each in isolation, tell a retrieval miss from a prompt failure, swap a model without rewriting business logic, and isolate irreversible actions as their own validated, gated step rather than burying a ledger write inside a synthesis call.",
    { example: "An HR team's policy assistant for 12,000 employees is designed as: intranet documents indexed for retrieval, Claude answering with citations, answers shown in the chat tool. A reviewer says the design is incomplete and asks for two additions. Candidates: a route for questions that no retrieved passage answers — decline and hand the question to an HR queue with an owner and a turnaround; a capture of thumbs-down ratings and unanswered questions into the evaluation set and a content-gap backlog; a larger context window so the whole handbook can be loaded on every call; an evaluator–optimiser loop that critiques every answer before display. The first two are right, because they supply the two missing stages — the failure path and the feedback loop. Loading the whole handbook is tempting because it seems to make retrieval misses impossible, but it only changes the processing stage, and it still leaves the system with no route when the handbook genuinely has no answer and no way to notice when its answers go stale." }
  ],

  "1.3": [
    { h: "What each rung buys" },
    "The ladder is ordered by how much control you hand to the model. An <b>augmented LLM</b> is one call enriched with retrieval, tools or memory; the model never chooses a sequence of actions. <b>Prompt chaining</b> runs a fixed sequence of calls with validation gates between them. <b>Routing</b> classifies the input and dispatches it to a specialised prompt or model. <b>Parallelisation</b> runs independent subtasks at once, or runs the same task several times and votes. <b>Orchestrator–workers</b> lets a central call break a task into subtasks at run time and delegate them. <b>Evaluator–optimiser</b> loops generation and critique for outputs where quality is worth extra calls. At the top, an <b>autonomous agent</b> decides its own steps and tools until it judges the task complete.",
    "Every rung up buys flexibility and pays for it in cost, latency, variance and debuggability. A workflow's cost and latency can be predicted before a request arrives and each step can be tested on its own; an agent's cannot, and its errors compound across steps. That asymmetry is why Anthropic's guidance is to start at the bottom and climb only when an evaluation shows the lower rung misses the bar.",
    { h: "Two facts pick the rung" },
    "Before choosing, establish two things. First, can the steps be enumerated before the request arrives? If yes, a predefined path exists and a workflow is available. If the next step depends on what the previous one found and the number of steps is unknown, only an agent can follow the problem. Second, how reversible are the actions? That decides how much autonomy is safe and what fences the design needs. Model tier and framework are chosen after these facts, not before.",
    "The exam's favourite misreading confuses <i>variety</i> with <i>unpredictability</i>. Documents that vary in layout, evidence that comes in several types, rules that change quarterly, or a conditional stage that fires for 12% of cases are all handled inside a workflow: layout variation by the extraction step, rule changes by retrieving the current rules, the optional stage by a classifier-triggered branch. None of them means the path cannot be written down. Unpredictability means an investigation where each finding decides the next lookup.",
    "The mirror-image error is hard-coding an investigation. Running every possible check on every case to look consistent wastes effort and still cannot adapt; a router with five fixed checklists breaks on the first case that fits none of them.",
    { h: "Choosing an agent is not choosing autonomy everywhere" },
    "Pattern and permissions are separate decisions. An agent justified by an unpredictable path can still have read-only tools, a bounded turn and cost budget, full trace logging, and a human who takes the irreversible or closing action. In regulated or high-consequence settings that combination — agency for exploration, a person for the decision — is usually the complete answer, and an option that grants write access \"so it can handle whatever comes up\" is usually the distractor.",
    { example: "A utility company wants Claude to draft replies to billing disputes. Every dispute needs the same data — meter readings, the applicable tariff and payment history — and the letters vary widely in tone, length and language. About 8% mention a medical dependency on electricity, which regulation says must be routed to a priority team. The options: an agent with meter, tariff and payment tools that plans each dispute, because the letters are so varied; a chained workflow that extracts the dispute's facts, fetches the three data sets in code, runs a classifier that sends medical-dependency cases down a priority branch, and drafts the reply for review; a supervisor with one subagent per data source; a single augmented call with all the data attached. The workflow is right. The steps are the same for every dispute; linguistic variety is absorbed by the extraction step, and the medical case is a known branch that regulation wants routed deterministically. The agent is the tempting answer because \"widely varied letters\" sounds like unpredictability, but variety in the input is not variety in the path. The single augmented call is closer than it looks, but it leaves a regulated routing decision to the model's discretion instead of making it an explicit, testable branch." }
  ],

  "1.4": [
    { h: "What isolation buys, and what it costs" },
    "A subagent runs in its own context window. It can read fifty documents, try three searches and discard two, and only its conclusion travels back to the orchestrator. That is the whole value of the pattern: the orchestrator's context stays small enough to reason over, and independent subagents can run at the same time — an orchestrator can dispatch several subagents in one turn and wait for them together rather than in sequence.",
    "The price is real. Tokens multiply, because each subagent re-reads what it needs. Coordination adds latency and failure points. And the orchestrator acts on a summary it cannot check, so one subagent's wrong or incomplete summary propagates silently into the synthesis. The pattern is the wrong answer when subtasks share mutable state or must happen strictly in order — there is nothing to parallelise and the hand-offs only lose information.",
    { h: "The hand-off contract is the design" },
    "Because nothing is inherited, the hand-off must carry everything the subagent needs: the findings so far or references to them, the source material, the constraints, the definitions that matter (what \"material\" means in this review) and the output schema. \"Write the summary of what we found\" fails because the subagent was never told what was found. The return contract matters just as much. Subagents should return structured, condensed results with the metadata synthesis needs — sources, dates, confidence — rather than raw material, which defeats the pattern by flooding the orchestrator's context. When a subagent fails, it should return structured error context (what failed, what it tried, any partial result) so the orchestrator can decide between retrying, trying an alternative and escalating.",
    "Around the contracts sit the orchestration concerns: a termination condition and a budget per agent, a rule for how errors propagate, and a trace ID that follows the request through every agent so a failure can be reconstructed. Routing all communication through the orchestrator — hub and spoke — is what makes that observability possible.",
    { h: "Split on boundaries, not on enthusiasm" },
    "The good reasons to split are distinct responsibilities, distinct tool sets or permissions, distinct data-access boundaries, and a need for parallel breadth or context isolation. Splitting along permission boundaries also limits what one compromised or misdirected agent can do. The bad reason is a single agent struggling with forty tools: that is a scoping problem, and the fix is a smaller, better-described tool set (see 3.1) before any second agent is introduced.",
    "Keep reasoning and irreversible action apart even inside a multi-agent design. An orchestrator that synthesises figures and writes them to the general ledger in the same turn has merged a judgement step with an unrecoverable one; the write belongs in its own validated, gated step. Finally, distinguish agent-to-agent from shared tools. When two independently owned systems each need their own reasoning and data — and one may not see the other's data — they exchange requests and verdicts agent to agent. When several agents simply need the same capability, expose it once through MCP (see 3.7). A shared MCP server over data that one side must not see breaches the boundary however well it is logged.",
    { example: "A due-diligence system has a supervisor and four subagents reviewing contracts, IP, employment and litigation files for an acquisition. The synthesis missed a change-of-control clause that should have been a deal-breaker: the contracts subagent mentioned it in prose, without the clause's effective date, and the employment subagent flagged similar clauses under a different label. The options are to merge everything into one agent so nothing is lost between contexts; to give the supervisor a larger context window and have subagents return their full source excerpts; to define a shared return schema (clause type, source document, effective date, severity) and include the deal team's definition of \"material\" in every hand-off; or to pass the supervisor's full history to each subagent so they share the same understanding. The schema and definition fix the actual fault, which is an under-specified contract in both directions. Passing the full history is the tempting answer because it sounds like shared understanding, but it discards the isolation that makes the pattern work and still does not tell any subagent what shape to return. Merging loses parallelism over hundreds of documents, and raw excerpts recreate the bloated supervisor context the design exists to avoid." }
  ],

  "1.5": [
    { h: "Choosing the axis" },
    "The three axes answer different questions. Decompose by <b>stage</b> when the work is a pipeline — extract, validate, decide, act — and each stage has a different failure mode. Decompose by <b>capability</b> when parts need different skills, tools or model tiers: research, drafting and review are different jobs. Decompose by <b>data boundary</b> when tenants, jurisdictions or permission scopes must never mix.",
    "When the axes compete, the data boundary takes precedence, because isolation has to be structural rather than promised. A shared retrieval index with a tenant identifier passed in the prompt relies on the model and the filter being right every time; separate per-tenant indexes and permissions make a cross-tenant leak impossible by construction. Stage and capability decomposition then nest inside each tenant, so you give up nothing by putting the boundary first.",
    { h: "Contracts make each piece testable" },
    "A unit is worth having only if it can be evaluated and replaced alone. That needs an input schema, an output schema and acceptance criteria. With them, you can measure the extraction step on its own eval set, move it to a cheaper tier and prove nothing regressed, or find that the failure sits in retrieval rather than in the prompt. Validation gates between chained steps turn a bad intermediate output into a stopped item instead of a corrupted final one.",
    "Deterministic checks belong in code, inside the decomposition, not in a prompt. Validating tariff codes against the official list, recomputing totals and checking dates are jobs a model can only approximate and code does exactly. Decomposition is also where you place human checkpoints: a gate on the step with the largest blast radius — declarations above a value threshold, payments, anything irreversible — rather than a review of everything.",
    { h: "Techniques, and when to stop" },
    "The techniques map onto the axes. Prompt chaining with gates suits fixed stages. Map-reduce handles inputs larger than the window: chunk, extract from each chunk to a schema, then consolidate — never truncate. Routing sends inputs to specialised prompts or models. Evaluator–optimiser loops fit outputs where quality justifies extra calls. For large reviews, per-item passes followed by an integration pass avoid the attention dilution of reading everything at once. When the scope itself is unknown, decomposition becomes dynamic: map the structure first, then build a prioritised plan and adapt it as findings arrive.",
    "Every cut costs a call, some latency, and a hand-off where detail can be lost — summaries compress numbers and dates into \"about\" and \"a few\". So decomposition has a stopping rule: split until each unit is independently testable, and split further only when a measurement shows a quality gain that pays for the extra latency and cost. A design with forty micro-steps and a review after each looks rigorous and usually is not.",
    { example: "A pharmaceutical company needs regulatory summaries of 600-page clinical study reports, with every number traceable to its table. Four proposals: forty micro-steps, one per report section, each followed by a top-tier review call; a map-reduce design that chunks by section, extracts findings to a schema with validation, consolidates them, runs a code check that every quoted figure matches its source table, and sends the draft to a medical writer for approval; one call on the largest window with the whole report; an agent that reads the report freely and writes the summary. The map-reduce design is right: each step has a contract, the numerical check is deterministic, and the human gate sits on the regulated output. The forty-step proposal is the tempting one because it looks the most controlled, but it multiplies latency and cost with no evidence of a quality gain and still lacks a deterministic figure check. The single call may not fit, dilutes attention across 600 pages, and leaves nothing to localise when a figure is wrong." }
  ],

  "1.6": [
    { h: "The pillar picks the metric" },
    "Stakeholders rarely say \"optimise accuracy\"; they say \"cut the cost of invoice queries\" or \"launch a product we cannot offer today\". Classifying that sentence into a pillar tells you what to measure and, just as importantly, what to hold as a constraint. <b>Efficiency</b> is time or throughput per unit of work — handle time, documents per night with the same team. <b>Cost</b> is spend per outcome. <b>Performance SLAs</b> are service guarantees: latency percentiles, availability, an accuracy floor. <b>Productivity</b> is output per person, typically developers or analysts. <b>Transformation</b> is a capability that does not exist yet.",
    "The pillar becomes a target before anything is built — a percentage reduction in handle time, a cost per ticket, a p95 latency — and the other axes become floors. That structure is what resolves the exam's hardest items, where every option is technically sound.",
    { h: "Floors, and the cost of optimising the wrong thing" },
    "Once the agreed quality floor is met, extra quality is not free value; it is spend on a metric nobody asked for. A verification pass that lifts accuracy from 93% to 96% while nearly tripling cost per query is a good engineering idea and a wrong answer when the sponsor's pillar is cost and the floor is 92%. Even a sensible-sounding compromise — apply the pass only above a monetary threshold and revisit the floor — is wrong for that question, because it changes the agreed criteria without evidence that they were inadequate.",
    "The same logic works for throughput. If an evaluator loop raises a rubric score but halves throughput and the pillar is throughput, prefer the change that improves quality without touching the pillar, such as targeted examples, and confine the heavy technique to the subset where quality matters more. Express cost with success in the denominator: a cheaper configuration that fails more often and triggers retries, escalations or human review costs more per completed task, whatever it costs per call.",
    { h: "When the pillar overrides the default" },
    "The challenge paper likes items where the named pillar flips the usual answer. When the pillar is transformation, capability completeness dominates: if the missing fifth of the capability is what defines the new product, the fuller design wins and its run cost and variance are presented as tracked risks with a budget cap. A cost-conscious executive in the room does not change the pillar the sponsor named. Legal and regulatory constraints override in the same way, but they override automation, not value — a signature requirement adds a gate without removing automated drafting.",
    "The business case is value minus build, run and risk. Run cost is more than model spend: include infrastructure, evaluation, human review and compliance effort, because an option that looks cheap on tokens can be expensive once its review queue is staffed.",
    { example: "A contact centre's sponsor names efficiency: cut average handle time from 11 to 7 minutes. A pilot that shows agents a summary of the customer's history has reached 8.5 minutes, and time studies show agents waiting several seconds for the summary and then scanning a long paragraph. Options: move the summary to the top tier, which raises summary quality by three points and adds four seconds of latency; switch to a short structured summary of three fields, generated while the call is being routed, on the current tier; add an evaluator–optimiser pass so every summary is critiqued before display; move to the cheapest tier to cut per-call cost by 60%, though summary quality falls below the agreed floor. The structured summary is right: it attacks the measured bottleneck in handle time and keeps quality above the floor. The top tier is the tempting answer because better summaries sound as if they must help, but it spends latency — the very thing the pillar measures — on quality that was not the constraint. The cheapest tier optimises cost, which nobody named, and breaks the floor." }
  ],

  "2.1": [
    { h: "Proportionality is a method, not a preference" },
    "Proportionality means the tier follows from the task's difficulty, volume and stakes, measured rather than assumed. A reliable method is to establish the quality ceiling on a strong model, then step down a tier at a time, re-running the evaluation set, and stop at the cheapest model that still clears the floor. The eval set decides; the tier names only tell you where to start looking.",
    "Volume and stakes pull in different directions, and the stem usually tells you which one dominates. Millions of short, well-bounded messages with a tight latency budget belong on the fastest adequate tier, where a one-point accuracy gain is not worth multiplying the bill. Forty high-value legal documents a day, with latency irrelevant, belong on the top tier: the absolute cost is negligible against what each document is worth. \"Cheapest\" and \"proportionate\" are different words, and so are \"strongest\" and \"safest\". Compensating for an under-sized tier by adding extended thinking is not proportionality either; it is the wrong tier with extra latency.",
    { h: "Routing puts each case on the tier it needs" },
    "Real workloads are rarely uniform. The routing pattern lets a cheap tier handle the bulk, a standard tier resolve what the first cannot, and the top tier take only the hard or low-confidence tail. Each tier, and the confidence threshold that moves a case between them, is validated on the evaluation set, because a threshold that escalates too little leaks errors and one that escalates too much quietly rebuilds the all-top-tier bill. Judge the result on cost per successfully completed task, not cost per call.",
    { h: "Versions are dependencies" },
    "A model upgrade behaves like any dependency upgrade: it can change output format, verbosity, refusal behaviour and tool-use tendencies. The characteristic symptom is a systematic shift — a preamble appearing before the JSON, answers growing longer — starting on a date when nobody deployed anything. That points at a floating alias moving underneath the system. The response has two halves: pin the previously validated dated model ID to stop the damage, then evaluate the new version against the regression set and migrate gradually behind a canary. Tightening the prompt treats the symptom and leaves the next release to repeat it. Structured outputs are good hardening but do not replace version governance.",
    "Migrations can break requests outright, not just shift behaviour. Current models return an error when sampling parameters such as temperature are set to a non-default value, so an old request that relied on temperature zero fails on the new model. Models also move through a published lifecycle — active, legacy, deprecated, retired — and Anthropic gives at least 60 days' notice to customers with active deployments before retirement. Partner clouds publish their own schedules, so check the platform you run on.",
    { h: "Features and platforms" },
    "Beyond the tier, check the feature axes the scenario names: context window size, extended and adaptive thinking, effort levels (lowering effort on a routine route trades a little depth for cost without changing the model), and vision and document input. Platform is a separate axis. When a constraint says inference must run inside the organisation's own cloud account and region with that cloud's identity and audit controls, the answer is Claude through Amazon Bedrock, Google Vertex AI or Microsoft Foundry in the required region. Storing an Anthropic API key in the cloud's secret store does not move where inference runs.",
    { example: "A multinational classifies 50,000 contract clauses every night into 20 categories; results are needed by morning and the agreed accuracy floor is 94%. On the evaluation set Haiku scores 91%, Sonnet 95% and Opus 96%. Options: Haiku through the Batch API because it is cheapest; Sonnet through the Batch API with a pinned model ID, escalating low-confidence clauses to Opus; Opus on every clause to be safe; Haiku with extended thinking to close the gap. The Sonnet design is right: it is the cheapest tier that clears the floor, the nightly window makes batch's discount free to take, pinning protects the result, and escalation covers the hard tail. Haiku is the tempting answer because the brief sounds cost-driven, but it misses the floor, and every misclassified clause becomes rework that raises cost per successful task. Opus buys one point the requirement does not need; thinking on Haiku is an unmeasured bet in place of the tier that already passes." }
  ],

  "2.2": [
    { h: "What goes where, and why" },
    "The split between system prompt and user turn is about durability and trust. The system prompt carries what is true for every request: role, scope, invariant rules, output contract, tone, refusal policy and the escalation rule. The user turn carries this request and its data. Put per-request data in the system prompt and you break caching and blur the line that protects against injection; put durable policy in the user turn and it becomes negotiable by whatever else arrives in that turn.",
    "Retrieved documents, user uploads and tool output are data, and they should look like data: wrapped in clearly labelled delimiters such as <document> tags, with an explicit instruction that content inside them is to be analysed and never obeyed. String-concatenating untrusted text into the instruction region is the prompting equivalent of SQL injection. Remember too that instructions do not transfer between surfaces: a convention Claude Code follows because it reads a project's CLAUDE.md has no effect in your API application unless your own system prompt says it.",
    { h: "Templates are code" },
    "A template is a parameterised prompt: a stable prefix of instructions and reference material, and a variable suffix where the request's data goes. Keep the variables at the end, so the prefix is identical across requests and can be cached (see 2.5). Version templates, review changes as you would code, and give each one an evaluation set, so that a wording change is a measured change rather than a hunch. Wording has side effects worth testing for: an instruction such as \"always verify the customer\" can make the model call a lookup tool when it has no reason to.",
    { h: "Guardrails in the prompt are a layer, not a lock" },
    "Prompt-level guardrails — stay in scope, refuse these requests, say \"unknown\" rather than guess — lower the frequency of bad behaviour. They do not bound it. When the requirement contains \"never\", \"must not\" or \"without exception\" and the action moves money, deletes data or produces a regulated output, the answer is a mechanism: remove or restrict the tool, check the condition in code before the action executes, or require an approval that the action cannot proceed without (see 5.1). Capital letters and repeated warnings are still only text the model weighs.",
    "The last guardrail is one that makes the model more honest rather than more constrained: give it an out. A schema that requires every field invites the model to invent a plausible value when the source lacks one. Allowing null or \"not found\", and telling the model to use it rather than guess, removes the pressure to fabricate. Structured outputs guarantee the shape of the answer, never its truth.",
    { example: "A wealth-management firm's assistant drafts trade instructions and can submit them through a tool. Compliance requires that no trade above a set value is submitted without an adviser's approval, without exception. The team proposes a capitalised system-prompt rule and three few-shot examples of the assistant pausing for approval; alternatives are removing the submit tool entirely, moving the rule into the user turn so it is closer to each request, or leaving the prompt rule in place while making the submit tool check the value in code and place anything above the threshold in an approval queue it cannot bypass. The last is right: \"without exception\" asks for a guarantee, and only code in the execution path provides one; the prompt rule stays as a helpful first layer. The prompt-and-examples proposal is tempting because it will usually work, but usually is not what compliance asked for. Removing the tool meets the rule by destroying the capability the business wanted, and the user-turn move weakens the rule rather than enforcing it." }
  ],

  "2.3": [
    { h: "Each rung fixes a different failure" },
    "The techniques are not strengths of the same medicine; each repairs a different fault. <b>Zero-shot</b> with precise instructions is enough when the model already does the task well: say what to do rather than what to avoid, specify the output shape, and replace vague adjectives with explicit criteria — \"flag a comment only if it contradicts the code\" rather than \"be conservative\". <b>Few-shot</b> repairs failures of format and of judgement on ambiguous cases, because an example shows the decision where a description only gestures at it. <b>Chain-of-thought</b> or extended thinking repairs failures of multi-step reasoning: chained arithmetic, many interacting constraints, cases where the answer depends on working something out.",
    "That makes the choice diagnostic. Look at where the errors on the evaluation set actually fall. Format errors call for examples or structured output. Errors clustered on one kind of ambiguous input call for an explicit rule plus an example or two of that kind. Errors on long reasoning chains call for reasoning before the answer. A classifier that is already accurate calls for nothing — and adding reasoning to a latency-critical, high-volume one spends its budget on every message for no measurable gain.",
    { h: "Examples that teach" },
    "Two to five diverse examples outperform a dozen near-identical ones. Include the edge cases: a set of easy examples teaches the model that hard cases do not happen. Keep every example in exactly the format you want back, and use them to show contrasts — acceptable versus problematic, escalate versus resolve. Examples are also a permanent cost: every one adds input tokens to every request, so each should earn its place.",
    { h: "Reasoning has a price, and so does emphasis" },
    "Reasoning costs output tokens, and output tokens are both priced higher than input and generated one at a time, so they dominate latency. Extended thinking is therefore the right move where latency is not a constraint and the failures are genuinely multi-step, and the wrong one on a 150 ms filter. On newer models, adaptive thinking with an effort level lets you tune how much reasoning a route gets rather than switching it wholly on or off.",
    "Capitals, \"be accurate\" and exclamation marks are not techniques. Structure — a format specification, criteria, examples, validation after the call — changes behaviour; intensity does not. Whatever you change, change one thing at a time against a fixed evaluation set and record it in a changelog. A rewrite that alters five things and lifts the score leaves you unable to say which change helped, or which one will break next release.",
    { example: "An insurer's triage prompt sorts incoming claims into six severity levels within a 400 ms budget and scores 93% on its evaluation set. Nearly all the errors are claims that describe both property damage and a minor injury, a combination the prompt never defines. The options are to enable extended thinking so the model can reason through mixed claims; to add twelve more examples of clear-cut claims; to add an explicit tie-break rule for mixed claims plus two examples of them; or to add \"CLASSIFY CAREFULLY\" at the top. The tie-break rule with targeted examples is right: the failure is a missing criterion, not a lack of reasoning depth, and the fix adds few tokens and no generated ones. Extended thinking is the tempting answer because it often raises accuracy, but it attacks the wrong cause and adds output latency the 400 ms budget cannot absorb. Clear-cut examples repeat what the model already gets right, and capitals change nothing." }
  ],

  "2.4": [
    { h: "Why more context can make things worse" },
    "As the number of tokens in the window grows, the model's ability to recall any particular item in it degrades — context rot. Position matters too: material at the start and end of a long input is used more reliably than material in the middle. So a large window is a budget, not a guarantee. Two failure modes follow. <b>Bloat</b> is accumulation: tool outputs, documents and superseded turns that are never removed. <b>Drift</b> is the objective being diluted as the window fills, so a long run gradually stops doing what it was asked. Their symptoms are recognisable: an agent ignoring a rule in its system prompt, re-reading files it has already seen, contradicting its own earlier decisions.",
    "A bigger window postpones those symptoms while making them worse. The API is stateless, so the whole history is sent — and paid for — on every request, and a larger window only allows more low-signal tokens to accumulate before anything forces a clean-up.",
    { h: "Curation, and what each technique preserves" },
    "The techniques differ in what they keep. <b>Pruning</b> removes consumed tool results; the platform's context editing clears old results and leaves a placeholder, and can also drop earlier thinking blocks. Clearing changes the prefix and so invalidates the cache from that point, which is why you clear a worthwhile amount at once rather than a little on every turn. <b>Compaction</b> summarises the earlier history into decisions, constraints and open items; the blocks it returns must be carried into the next request, so a loop that appends only the response text silently loses the compacted state. <b>Memory</b> — the memory tool the model calls — stores facts outside the window and reads them back, even in a later conversation; pairing it with pruning is what makes pruning safe. <b>Just-in-time retrieval</b> keeps references such as file paths and record IDs and reloads detail on demand. <b>Isolation</b> pushes detailed exploration into a subagent that returns only its conclusion.",
    "Summaries have a known weakness: numbers, dates and amounts are condensed into \"about\" and \"a partial refund\". Keep transactional facts in a structured case-facts block outside the summarised history, trim tool output to the fields that matter before it enters the context, place long documents before the question, and keep critical rules in the system prompt.",
    { h: "Accounting in tokens" },
    "Budgets are counted in tokens, not words. Each response's <code>usage</code> reports input, output, cache-write and cache-read tokens; read it per request rather than discovering the total on an invoice, and count tokens before sending when a budget must be enforced. Cap <code>max_tokens</code> deliberately: output and thinking tokens cost several times what input costs and are generated serially, so verbosity is both the most expensive habit and the largest latency term. When an input will not fit, decompose it — chunk and map-reduce, or a multi-step workflow — and never truncate silently.",
    { example: "A support agent handles cases that run over several days. By day three its context is dominated by CRM lookups that return 40 fields each, of which five matter, and after an automatic compaction it refers to the refund agreed on day one as \"a partial refund\" and offers a different figure. The options are to move to a model with a larger context window; to turn compaction off so the original turns are kept verbatim; to trim CRM results to the relevant fields before they enter the context and keep agreed amounts, dates and commitments in a structured case-facts block that is carried forward outside the summarised history; or to re-inject the full system prompt every ten turns. The third is right: it removes the bloat at source and protects exactly the facts that summarisation blurs. Turning compaction off is the tempting answer because it restores the exact figure, but it brings back the bloat that made compaction necessary. The larger window defers the problem and raises the cost of every call; re-injecting the prompt adds tokens without protecting any fact." }
  ],

  "2.5": [
    { h: "Three problems, three mechanisms" },
    "The three reuse mechanisms are easy to blur because all of them \"avoid repetition\". Separate them by the problem they solve. <b>Prompt caching</b> solves cost and time-to-first-token when the same tokens are sent on request after request. <b>Modular prompts</b> solve governance when the same logic is shared by several applications or teams and must not diverge. <b>Skills</b> solve context load when many procedures are needed only some of the time. A fourth mechanism sits next to them and is often offered as a distractor: an MCP server provides capabilities and access to live systems, not instructions (see 3.7).",
    { h: "How caching actually matches" },
    "Caching reuses an exact prefix, in a fixed order: tool definitions, then the system prompt, then messages. A change anywhere invalidates everything after it, so a changed tool definition invalidates the whole request below it. Cache breakpoints — up to four — mark the boundaries between content that changes at different rates. Reads cost a small fraction of normal input and cut time-to-first-token; writes cost more than normal input, which is why caching content that never repeats raises the bill. The default lifetime is five minutes with a longer option, there is a minimum cacheable length, and caching works on ordinary synchronous requests, not only in batch.",
    { code: "tools            stable, rarely changes\nsystem prompt    stable per template version\nreference docs   stable per product or policy version\n---- breakpoint ----\nconversation     grows each turn\nuser message     changes every request", label: "Static first, volatile last" },
    "The diagnostic is in the <code>usage</code> object. Cache writes on nearly every request with almost no reads means something inside the prefix changes every time: a customer message placed before the manual, a timestamp at the top of the system prompt, a JSON blob serialised in a different key order, a tool list assembled in a different sequence. The fix is ordering, not more breakpoints. And position is the only thing that makes content cacheable — moving a policy into few-shot examples changes its format, not its cacheability.",
    { h: "Modular prompts and Skills" },
    "Modular prompts treat shared prompt logic as a library. Policy, format and refusal blocks are versioned centrally, each with its own evaluation set and changelog; applications reference a version, and a block change cannot ship until its evaluation passes. That is what stops one team adding a disclaimer, another rewording refusals and a third application running a six-month-old copy.",
    "A Skill packages a procedure as a <code>SKILL.md</code> with supporting files, and its description determines when it is loaded: the procedure enters context only when a task matches, which is progressive disclosure. Twelve procedures inlined in every system prompt cost tokens on every request and invite the model to confuse them; caching lowers the price of that flood but leaves the flood. In Claude Code, a skill can run in a forked subagent context so verbose work stays out of the main session, and can be limited to an allowed set of tools. Universal conventions stay in CLAUDE.md; situational procedures become Skills.",
    { example: "A bank's internal assistant sends, on every request: a line with the current date and time, the tool definitions, a 6,000-token system prompt, a 20,000-token compliance handbook, nine operating procedures inlined at 14,000 tokens, and then the conversation. The bill is high, cache reads are close to zero, and users report the assistant mixing up procedures. The options are to add more cache breakpoints around the handbook; to move the date line to the end of the request so the tools, system prompt and handbook form a stable cached prefix, and convert the nine procedures into Skills with precise descriptions; to put the procedures into an MCP server the assistant can query; or to move the handbook into few-shot examples. The second is right: the timestamp at the very start invalidates everything after it, so moving it restores the prefix, and Skills take the procedures out of context until they are needed, which also addresses the confusion. More breakpoints are the tempting answer because they sound like more caching, but every breakpoint sits after the changing timestamp, so none of them can ever be read. MCP carries access, not procedures, and the few-shot move changes format rather than position." }
  ],

  "3.1": [
    { h: "Why tool count is an architecture variable" },
    "Every tool you give an agent is sent with every request: its <code>name</code>, <code>description</code> and <code>input_schema</code> are input tokens you pay for whether or not the tool is called. More importantly, the model chooses between tools by reading those descriptions, so each extra tool is one more candidate in every single selection decision. Two tools described as \"Fetch data\" are ambiguous to any reader, human or model, and no amount of model capability resolves an ambiguity that is in the interface itself.",
    "That is why the lesson calls bloat an architecture problem. The four effects in the points compound: a long, overlapping tool list degrades selection, inflates the cost and latency of every request, gives an injected instruction more things it could try to drive, and gives an auditor more code paths to review. The CCAR-F guide puts the reliability point bluntly — an agent with 18 tools selects less reliably than one with the 4–5 its role needs, and agents holding tools outside their specialisation tend to misuse them.",
    { h: "The order of the fix" },
    "The fixes in the points are listed in the order you should apply them, and the order is what the exam tests. Removal comes first because it is the cheapest change and it improves all four effects at once. It also makes every later measurement meaningful: a mis-selection rate measured over 31 tools tells you little about how 6 well-scoped tools will behave. So remove, then re-measure, and only then decide whether the remaining problem is descriptions (rename, rewrite with what it returns, when to use it and when not to, consolidate primitives into the operation the agent actually performs), structure (split into specialists) or scale (routing, or deferred loading behind tool search — see 3.8).",
    "Skipping straight to a rewrite of 31 descriptions or a six-agent split is not wrong in kind, but it is wrong in sequence: you spend effort polishing and distributing tools that should not exist. The official-style item on a bank's branch agent rewards exactly this ordering — remove what the role never uses, measure again, then decide.",
    { h: "Least privilege is about capabilities, not just credentials" },
    "Least privilege is usually taught as a property of keys and accounts. For agents it applies one level up: a tool the role never needs is a privilege, and the control that matches it is removal. Logging is a detective control — it tells you after the event that a deletion happened. A confirmation hook is a compensating control — it keeps the capability and adds a gate that can be approved by mistake or by habit. Only absence guarantees the action cannot occur, which is why \"never\" in a stem points at removal.",
    "Removal is not the whole security story. The tools that remain still need authorisation enforced in the backend on the end user's identity (3.2); hiding a tool reduces what the agent can attempt, while the backend check decides what this user may do. For a new deployment the sequencing item is explicit: scope the tool set to the role and confirm each tool enforces the caller's permissions before exposure. Sixty days of telemetry is how you prune an existing system, not a precondition for launching a new one.",
    { h: "How the exam frames it" },
    "Expect a long stem with numbers: tool count, tools actually used, mis-selection rate, tokens per request spent on definitions, and a telemetry line saying some tools were never called. The audit inputs in the points are the evidence the stem is handing you. The distractors are the compensations: a larger model, a bigger context window, a confirmation hook, more logging, or prompt caching to make the definitions cheaper. Caching lowers the price of the definitions; it does nothing for selection or attack surface.",
    { example: "An insurer's claims agent holds 26 tools. Adjusters only read claims, search policy documents and add notes, but the agent also carries <code>bulk_update_policy</code> and <code>issue_payment</code>, neither called in 90 days, plus three search tools whose descriptions differ by one word. Mis-selection is 14%. The options: add a PreToolUse confirmation on the two write tools; move to a stronger model; rewrite all 26 descriptions; or remove the out-of-role and unused tools, merge the three searches into one well-described tool, and re-measure. The confirmation hook is tempting because it feels safe and keeps flexibility, but it is a compensating control on a capability the role never needs, and it leaves mis-selection untouched. The stronger model compensates for ambiguity that is in the interface. The full rewrite polishes tools that should be deleted. Removal plus consolidation, then a fresh measurement, fixes the cause on all four axes and is the defensible first move." }
  ],

  "3.2": [
    { h: "Where the end user's identity gets lost" },
    "The confused deputy is a specific shape of failure. A user with narrow rights talks to an agent; the agent calls a tool; the tool calls a backend using a service credential that can read everything. The backend sees only the service account, so it answers with the service account's rights. The user never had permission to read a colleague's salary, but the deputy did, and the deputy did what it was asked. Nothing in that chain is a bug in the model — the identity simply never reached the place where access is decided.",
    "This is why the lesson insists the agent acts with the end user's effective permissions. The fix is to carry identity through: the tool call carries a token that represents the user (OAuth on-behalf-of, or a scoped token issued for that user), and the backend evaluates access on that identity, on every call, at the moment of the action. An earlier step that checked the user does not license a later step to assume it.",
    { h: "Why a prompt rule is not a control" },
    "A system prompt that says \"only return records belonging to the requesting employee\" shapes behaviour; it does not enforce anything. It fails on indirect phrasing (\"compare my salary with the team average, listing each\"), on injected instructions in retrieved content, and on any day the model reasons its way to an exception. The model sits above the enforcement layer, not inside it. When a penetration test gets past a prompt rule, strengthening the wording only changes which phrasing succeeds next.",
    "The same logic removes several other tempting answers. A larger model is not an authorisation mechanism. Monitoring query volume and audit logging are detective. Rotating a shared token and shortening its lifetime improves hygiene but leaves every caller holding the same full rights, so the gap survives the rotation. The correct remediation order in the one-sprint item is to move authentication and authorisation into the tools and the retrieval layer with user-scoped credentials, and then delete the prompt filter as redundant.",
    { h: "Credentials, environments and tenants" },
    "The typical gaps in the points share a root cause: a credential whose scope is wider than the caller's. A shared API key across environments lets a development mistake reach production data, so keep separate keys and Workspaces per environment, scoped to the narrowest one that works, rotated on a schedule and revoked on suspicion. Secrets never live in prompts, logs, memory files or checked-in MCP configuration; a shared <code>.mcp.json</code> references them through environment-variable expansion so the configuration is shared and the secret is not. Per-tenant isolation in a retrieval store is authorisation too, and it belongs in a mandatory query filter or a separate index (3.6), not in an instruction.",
    { h: "MCP transports and trust" },
    "The transport tells you what kind of trust you are extending. A remote MCP server on Streamable HTTP can authenticate each user with OAuth, which is what makes per-user enforcement inside the server possible. A local stdio server is a process the client launches, and it runs with the privileges of whoever started it — so it is trusted code, to be reviewed and pinned like any dependency. Switching a multi-user HR server to stdio is a distractor: it changes where the server runs, not who it believes is calling.",
    { example: "A finance copilot uses an MCP server over the general ledger. The server authenticates with one service account that can read every cost centre. The system prompt is told the user's cost centre and instructed to stay within it. A department manager asks \"how does my travel spend compare with Marketing's, line by line?\" and receives Marketing's line items. Options: add refusal examples to the prompt; rotate the service token and alert on unusual query volume; move the server to stdio; or issue a user-scoped token on behalf of the signed-in manager and enforce cost-centre access inside the tool. The prompt fix is tempting because the leak looks like a wording failure, but the model was never the right place for the check. Rotation and alerts keep the superset credential. Stdio changes nothing about identity. Only the on-behalf-of token with enforcement in the tool ties authorisation to the end user, after which the prompt instruction can go." }
  ],

  "3.3": [
    { h: "Every step has a price" },
    "Each thing you add to a pipeline to improve answers — another retrieval hop, a re-ranker, a verification pass, extended or adaptive thinking at a higher effort level, a stronger model — adds time, and most add cost. Steps that run in sequence add their latencies, so a pipeline that was comfortably inside its SLA can breach it with one well-intentioned addition. The SLA is also a percentile: a p95 target means the slow tail is what you are judged on, not the average.",
    "The levers in the points pull in opposite directions, and the job is to spend latency where it buys the most accuracy. Toward speed: a smaller model on the path, streaming so users see output early, prompt caching on a stable prefix to cut time-to-first-token, parallel tool calls instead of sequential ones, fewer hops, and a tighter <code>max_tokens</code> — output length drives both latency and cost. Eager input streaming on a tool with large inputs removes the pause while the server buffers the arguments, but it moves validation of those arguments to your handler, so it is a trade, not a free win.",
    { h: "Reading the numbers" },
    "Treat the stem's figures as a filter, then a choice. First apply every hard constraint the scenario states — p95 SLA, quality floor, cost ceiling — and strike out every option that fails any of them. Then choose among the survivors, usually the cheapest or simplest that clears the bar. If nothing survives, the answer is to restructure the path, not to pick the least bad breach. In the review item with a 1,200 ms SLA and a 92% floor, the option with the highest accuracy fails the SLA, the baseline fails the floor, and only the re-ranker satisfies both; \"highest accuracy\" was never the criterion.",
    "Both deltas must be measured, not asserted: the accuracy gain on the evaluation set and the p95 change under realistic load. A configuration that wins on a demo set of easy questions may add nothing on the hard tail, and a latency figure taken from a quiet afternoon says little about peak traffic.",
    { h: "Selective escalation" },
    "When the accuracy gain lives in a small subset of traffic, apply the expensive step only there. Route queries detected as hard, or whose first pass comes back low-confidence, to the stronger model or the thinking-enabled path, and keep the fast path as the default. This captures most of the gain while protecting the percentile — but only if the escalated share is small. If 3% of requests take the slow path, the 95th percentile can still fall on the fast path; if 10% do, p95 lands on the slow path and the SLA is breached anyway. That is why the correct options always add \"verify that p95 stays within the target\".",
    "The same reasoning answers the second trap: a verification pass on every request when only the high-stakes subset needs it pays the latency cost on the 95% of traffic that gains nothing.",
    { h: "What is not the architect's call" },
    "Two moves appear as distractors in almost every trade-off item. The Batch API trades latency for a 50% discount, with most batches finishing within an hour and a 24-hour ceiling, so it is never the answer for anything a person or a real-time control is waiting on. And accepting an SLA breach unilaterally — \"document it as accepted risk\", \"it can be renegotiated\" — is not a configuration decision. Changing an SLA is a stakeholder decision made with evidence; the architect's job is to find the configuration that meets the constraints as given, or to show with numbers that none can.",
    { example: "An agent-assist feature for a contact centre has a p95 SLA of 1,500 ms and a quality floor of 85%. Measured on the eval set: baseline 82% at 900 ms; plus re-ranking 86% at 1,250 ms; plus a verification pass 88% at 1,700 ms; plus a stronger model 90% at 2,100 ms. The operations director asks for the 90% option because \"agents trust accurate suggestions\". Filter first: the baseline misses the floor; verification and the stronger model miss the SLA. Only re-ranking meets both, so it is the justified default. The stronger model is tempting because its accuracy gain is largest, but choosing it means breaching a stated constraint without authority. If the stem had added that a known 4% of calls (account closures, say) carry the real risk, the better answer would become re-ranking by default with verification routed only to that subset, with p95 checked after the change." }
  ],

  "3.4": [
    { h: "Why the trace is the unit" },
    "In a multi-step system the step that fails is rarely the step that shows it. A retrieval that returns the wrong policy produces a fluent summary of the wrong policy, which a classifier then labels correctly for what it was given, which a router sends to the wrong queue. Every service returns success; every service's own log looks clean. The claims-pipeline item with three weeks of fruitless investigation is exactly this: four stores, four local IDs, no way to follow one claim from entry to queue.",
    "A correlation ID generated at the entry point and propagated through every model call, tool call, retrieval and downstream service is what turns four logs into one story. With it you can pull the complete path of a misrouted item and see where the content first went wrong. Without it, longer retention only keeps more unjoinable records, and consolidating the services is a rewrite that solves a logging problem at enormous cost.",
    { h: "What to record, and what not to" },
    "The per-step record in the points is chosen so that any failure can be attributed. Prompt version and model ID tell you whether a change caused it; the API <code>request-id</code> lets you correlate with the provider; token usage, including the cache read and write figures, explains cost and shows whether caching works; <code>stop_reason</code> distinguishes a finished answer from a truncated one or a refusal; tool calls with arguments, latency and outcome show what the agent did; retrieval hits with document IDs and scores show what it was given.",
    "At millions of requests a day you cannot keep every payload, and you should not. The pattern is metadata and traces for every request, full payloads sampled, redacted at the point of writing, with access control and retention limits on the sample. Logging raw prompts that carry personal or health data into a general-purpose log platform creates the least protected copy of your most sensitive data. The argument \"we need every payload to replay non-deterministic behaviour\" sounds rigorous, but the useful replay rule is narrower: replay the exact recorded request and inspect the raw response — if it is right, the fault is in your integration; if it is wrong, the fault is upstream in prompt, context, tools or model.",
    { h: "Failures that return 200" },
    "The first trap — monitoring only HTTP errors and latency — fails because most LLM failures are successful HTTP calls. A response cut off at <code>max_tokens</code> returns 200 with invalid JSON inside. A streaming response can carry an error event after the 200 status. A server tool such as web search reports its failure as an error object inside the tool-result block of an ordinary success response, so exception counters record nothing. A retrieval that finds nothing relevant, a model that hallucinates, a routing decision that is wrong: all 200.",
    "That is why the points add quality signals alongside the operational ones: evaluation scores on sampled traffic, user feedback, refusal and fallback rates, HITL queue size, tool mis-selection. Some operational metrics double as diagnostics — a cache hit rate that falls to zero means something inside the prefix changes on every request. The monitoring programme that consumes these signals is 4.6; here the question is what must be captured so those signals exist.",
    { example: "An invoice-intake pipeline runs OCR, extraction, validation and posting to the ERP across three teams' services. About 3% of invoices post with the wrong amount; no service reports an error. Options: alert on 5xx rates; store every full prompt and response in the shared log platform for replay; propagate a correlation ID through all four steps with per-step metadata (prompt version, model ID, stop reason, extracted fields, validation outcome) plus sampled, redacted payloads scored for quality; or extend retention. Alerting on 5xx sees nothing, because every call succeeded. Full payload logging is the tempting answer because it promises perfect replay, but it copies supplier bank details and personal data into a general store at full volume and still cannot join the steps without a shared ID. Retention has the same joining problem. The correlated trace with sampled, redacted payloads is the only option that restores attribution at acceptable cost and risk." }
  ],

  "3.5": [
    { h: "Two sides of the model" },
    "The pre/post split in the points is not a filing exercise; it tells you where a defect can be fixed. Pre-processing acts on the corpus and decides what can ever be found: if a table was split from its header at ingestion, or personal data was embedded, no amount of later work undoes it. Post-processing acts on what retrieval returned or what the model produced and decides what of the found material reaches the model and the user. The classification test is always the same: is the step operating on source documents on the way into the index, or on hits and answers on the way out?",
    "Two items catch people because the same verb appears on both sides. Deduplicating near-identical source documents so the index holds one copy is pre-processing; deduplicating near-identical hits before assembling context is post-processing. Extracting tenant and date metadata during ingestion is pre-processing; filtering retrieved chunks by that metadata before assembly is post-processing.",
    { h: "Choosing a chunking strategy" },
    "Pick the chunk by asking what the unit of meaning is in this document. For a contract it is the clause; for a manual it is the procedure or section; for code it is the function or class; for a pricing sheet it is the table with its header. When the document tells you its own structure, use it — structure-aware chunking, with overlap where a unit's meaning leans on its neighbour. Recursive splitting is the sensible default when structure is weak, semantic chunking earns its compute cost on long unstructured prose, and fixed-size chunking is acceptable only where nothing much spans a boundary.",
    "Some corpora need more than a good boundary. Dense contracts cross-reference clauses by number and keep definitions elsewhere, so clause-level chunks need metadata (contract ID, clause number, section) and a retrieval step that pulls the referenced clauses and definitions. When precision and context conflict, parent-child separates the unit you match on from the unit you pass to the model: match a sentence, hand over the section. Document summaries plus chunks let you route to the right document before searching inside it.",
    { h: "Size, top-k and the precision trade" },
    "Chunk size and top-k together set how many tokens reach the model and how focused they are. Larger chunks mean each hit carries more context but also more irrelevant text, and at a fixed token budget fewer distinct hits fit. The tempting fix for procedures that stop halfway — raise chunk size to 1,200 tokens and increase top-k — treats a boundary problem as a size problem, dilutes relevance and inflates cost. Tune the two jointly and judge them with retrieval metrics such as recall@k and precision (3.6), not by reading a few answers.",
    { h: "Indexing and versioning" },
    "Vector search finds meaning, keyword search finds exact strings, and a corpus with both needs a hybrid index. Metadata filters handle tenancy, recency and access. The index is tied to the embedding model that produced it: vectors from two models do not live in the same space, so a collection that is 60% new-model and 40% old-model returns irrelevant or missing hits for the old portion. The fix is to version the index with its embedding model and re-embed the whole collection on any change. This is why the refresh symptom — confident, wrong answers right after a document refresh, with model and latency unchanged — sends you to retrieval first: the only thing that changed is the data path, so temperature and model tier are red herrings.",
    { example: "An insurer's product assistant indexes policy booklets with fixed 400-token chunks. Customers are quoted the wrong benefit limits, and inspection shows the retrieved chunks contain rows of a benefits table without the header saying which plan tier each column belongs to. The team proposes a stronger model \"that can infer the columns\". Other options: double the chunk size; lower temperature; or chunk structure-aware so each table stays whole with its header, tagged with product and booklet version, using parent-child so a matched row brings its full table. The stronger model is tempting because the failure looks like a reasoning error, but the information that disambiguates the columns was removed at ingestion and is not in the context at all. Doubling chunk size may or may not keep a table intact and dilutes every other query. Temperature is irrelevant. Structure-aware chunking with metadata fixes the defect where it was created." }
  ],

  "3.6": [
    { h: "Two inputs to the decision" },
    "The strategy follows from two things you can observe before building anything: what the data looks like and what people ask of it. Embeddings are good at one job — finding passages that mean something similar to the query. They are weak at exact identifiers, because <code>E-4471</code> and <code>E-4417</code> sit close together in meaning while being entirely different faults, and they cannot aggregate at all. \"Embed everything and hope\" fails whenever the data or the query needs a job embeddings do not do.",
    { h: "Data shapes that are not documents" },
    "Relational data is the clearest case. A question like \"refunds by region in Q2 versus Q1\" is a sum over rows; retrieving the 200 rows most similar to the question and asking the model to add them up gives an answer computed over an arbitrary sample, stated with confidence. The right design gives the model a query tool — parameterised SQL or a semantic layer — that runs the aggregate in the database and returns a compact result. Parameterised queries also keep the model from composing arbitrary SQL, and the tool can enforce the caller's permissions (3.2). Pre-computed summaries per region and month look like a shortcut but cannot cover ad-hoc combinations.",
    "Other shapes follow the same logic. Codes, part numbers and names want keyword or exact matching; code wants structure-aware chunks by function or file; long prose wants semantic or hierarchical chunks. A corpus that mixes them — policy prose alongside thousands of error codes — wants hybrid retrieval with re-ranking, so each query is served by the matcher that suits it.",
    { h: "Query patterns change the retrieval loop" },
    "The query log tells you how retrieval should run, not just what to index. Precise lookups want metadata filters plus keyword matching. Exploratory questions want semantic search followed by re-ranking. Multi-hop questions need iterative or agentic retrieval, because the term you need for the second search only appears in the result of the first. Comparisons across documents need retrieval per document followed by synthesis; a single top-k over the whole corpus tends to fill with passages from whichever document matches best, leaving the other side of the comparison missing. Repeated identical prefixes are not a retrieval problem at all — that is caching.",
    { h: "Order of work and measurement" },
    "The sequencing item is precise about what comes first: profile the corpus (structure, tables, identifiers) and the real query log, and define retrieval metrics such as recall@k, precision and groundedness. Only then choose chunking and index type, then tune top-k and thresholds, then work on the generation prompt. Choosing the embedding model first, or running an end-to-end generation eval as the baseline, puts a downstream decision ahead of the facts that should drive it.",
    "Measuring retrieval separately from generation is what localises failures. If the passage that answers the question is not in the top-k, no prompt change can fix the answer; if it is there and the answer is still wrong, the problem is generation. Judging RAG only by final answer quality leaves you unable to tell which half to change. Tenant and access rules sit in this layer too: a mandatory tenant filter on every query, or separate indexes. A prompt instruction, a larger model or a higher similarity threshold cannot enforce isolation, because isolation is enforced where data is fetched, not where it is read.",
    { example: "A pharmaceutical company's regulatory assistant covers submission dossiers (long prose), a product master table, and batch records keyed by batch number. The query log shows about 40% \"what is the status of batch 24-0917\", 35% \"compare the stability data in submissions A and B\", and 25% open questions about regulatory history. The proposal is a single vector index over everything with top-k raised to 20 \"to be safe\". The better design routes by pattern: batch and product lookups go to exact matching or a query tool over the master and batch data; comparisons retrieve from each submission separately and then synthesise; open questions use semantic search with re-ranking; recall@k is defined per pattern before tuning. Raising top-k is tempting because it seems to guarantee the answer is somewhere in the context, but it does not make batch numbers match exactly, it still lets one submission crowd out the other, and it multiplies the tokens sent on every request." }
  ],

  "3.7": [
    { h: "Three mechanisms, three different questions" },
    "The three options are not ranked by power; each answers a different question. A direct API or CLI integration answers \"how does this one application reach this one system?\" MCP answers \"how do many clients reach the same capability without each rebuilding it?\" Agent-to-agent answers \"how do two systems that each reason for themselves, under separate ownership, delegate work to each other?\" The lesson's claim that the choice turns on reuse and ownership boundaries rather than capability follows directly: any of the three can technically reach an ERP, so capability never decides the item.",
    "A compact decision rule: first ask whether you are sharing a capability or delegating judgement. If it is judgement across an ownership boundary, it is agent-to-agent. If it is a capability, count the consumers — one application with a fixed connection points to a direct integration; several applications, teams or surfaces, maintained by someone other than each consumer, points to MCP. If the gap is know-how rather than reach, it is neither — that is a Skill (2.5).",
    { h: "What MCP costs and what it buys" },
    "An MCP server exposes tools, resources and prompts that any compliant client can discover when it connects. What that buys is reuse and independence: one team owns the integration, its authentication and its observability, and every client benefits from each fix. What it costs is a protocol, a deployment and an auth surface. For a single dashboard calling one fixed endpoint, those costs buy nothing, which is the first trap.",
    "Transport and client details can decide a stem. A local stdio server is launched by the client and runs with the user's privileges; a remote server on Streamable HTTP authenticates with OAuth, which is what a multi-user enterprise integration needs. In Claude Code a project-scoped <code>.mcp.json</code> is committed so the team shares one definition, with secrets supplied through environment-variable expansion. Through the API's MCP connector, servers are attached by URL only, so a server that must serve API applications has to be remote, and only its tools are exposed. For standard systems such as GitHub or Jira, an existing server usually beats building your own.",
    { h: "Agent-to-agent is about judgement" },
    "Agent-to-agent communication fits when each side has its own reasoning, context, data boundary and owner, and what passes between them is a proposal and a verdict rather than a record. Procurement proposing a purchase and finance approving it is the canonical shape: neither team should hand the other its data, and each must apply its own policy. The exchange needs a defined contract — what is sent, what comes back, how refusal and failure are expressed — because you are joining two systems you do not control end to end.",
    "Its costs are real: each hand-off adds model calls, latency and tokens, and the reasoning on the far side is opaque to the caller. Using it where a shared tool would do — five applications asking each other's agents for inventory figures — pays those costs for a lookup. The opposite error also appears: a single shared MCP server holding both procurement and finance data erases the boundary agent-to-agent exists to respect.",
    { h: "Evaluating on the criteria" },
    "The criteria in the points map onto the stem's details. Reuse and the maintaining team decide between direct and MCP. Ownership boundaries decide whether delegation is needed at all. The auth model and failure isolation favour MCP for shared access and agent-to-agent where one side's failure must not expose the other's data. Latency overhead and observability count against every extra layer, which is why the simplest mechanism that meets the need wins.",
    { example: "A logistics group has three needs. A nightly job in one service fetches carrier rates from a single endpoint nobody else uses. Six teams want shipment tracking inside Claude Code, the desktop app and two API-based services, and a platform team will own it. The customs-compliance system, with its own agent, owner and restricted data, must approve export shipments proposed by the logistics agent. A reviewer suggests one MCP server for all three \"because MCP is the enterprise standard\". The correct mapping is a direct integration for the rates job, a remote MCP server on Streamable HTTP with OAuth for tracking (remote because API clients attach servers by URL), and an agent-to-agent hand-off with a defined contract for customs approval. The single-server answer is tempting for its uniformity, but it adds protocol overhead to the one-off job and would put customs data behind a server the logistics side can query directly." }
  ],

  "3.8": [
    { h: "What monolithic context costs at scale" },
    "A monolithic request loads every tool definition, document and procedure every time. At low volume that is merely wasteful; at enterprise volume three costs compound. Price: every token is billed on every request, and the retail item's 41,000 tokens across 600,000 requests a day is a large standing charge for content most requests never use. Quality: as the context fills, the model's ability to recall any particular item degrades, and every tool definition is another candidate in every selection, so mis-selection rises. Exposure: every loaded document is text the model reads, and a product description carrying injected instructions influences requests that never needed that document.",
    "This is why the tempting fixes miss. Prompt caching bills the stable prefix at a fraction of the input rate and cuts time-to-first-token, so it genuinely helps cost and latency, but the model still reads all 41,000 tokens, still chooses among 38 tools and still sees the injected text. A larger context window makes the problem more affordable without making it smaller. Removing required policy documents to save tokens loses content the task needs.",
    { h: "The mechanisms of disclosure" },
    "Progressive disclosure keeps a small always-on core and makes everything else findable. The platform offers several mechanisms and the points name them. Deferred tool loading behind a tool-search tool keeps most definitions out of context until the model searches for them; search comes in regex and BM25 variants, and the API refuses a request that defers everything — the search tool must stay loaded and at least one other tool must too, or the model has nowhere to start. Skills keep only their name and description resident and load the body when a task matches. Just-in-time retrieval stores references and fetches content on demand. MCP resources can serve as a catalog, so the agent knows what exists without exploratory calls, and subagents isolate exploration so only conclusions return.",
    "What belongs in the core is decided by one question: must this apply to every request regardless of what the model goes looking for? Identity, safety rules and the handful of tools used on most requests qualify. Safety rules in particular must never depend on discovery — a rule the model has to find is a rule it can fail to find. A stable core is also the natural cacheable prefix, so the two techniques combine rather than compete.",
    { h: "What progressive costs" },
    "Discovery is not free. Each search or load is an extra step with latency and another point of failure, and the whole design depends on the quality of what is searched: tool descriptions, Skill descriptions and document indexes. A capability described as \"Fetch data\" was already hard to select when it was always loaded; behind a search it may never be found at all, which is the second trap. Moving to progressive disclosure therefore includes rewriting descriptions to say what each tool returns and when to use it, and monitoring how often a needed capability was not discovered.",
    { h: "Proportionality" },
    "Progressive is the preference at scale, not a rule for every system. A system with three tools, 1,800 tokens of documents and 200 requests a day gains nothing from a discovery layer and pays its complexity. The professional answer there is to keep the monolith, record the decision, and name the triggers — tool count, document size, request volume — that would reopen it. Stems give you exactly those three numbers so you can tell which side of the line the system is on.",
    { example: "A bank's internal operations assistant carries 55 tools across six back-office systems and 14 procedures, about 32,000 tokens per request at 300,000 requests a day. Mis-selection is 12%. Options: cache the whole context; move to a model with a larger window; delete the procedures and rely on general knowledge; or keep a core of identity, safety rules and the five most-used tools, defer the rest behind tool search with rewritten descriptions, package the procedures as Skills and retrieve documents on demand. Caching is the tempting answer because it visibly cuts the bill, but it leaves the 55-way selection and the exposure untouched. The larger window changes neither. Deleting procedures loses required knowledge. The progressive design is right — and if the team's first draft defers every tool including the search tool, expect the API to reject it: the core must contain the entry point." }
  ],

  "4.1": [
    { h: "Why five axes and not one" },
    "An LLM system is a product with an SLA, not a model with a benchmark score. The five axes exist because each one fails independently of the others: a prompt change can raise accuracy while doubling p95 latency, a cheaper tier can hold accuracy while refusing more legitimate requests, and a new document-upload feature can leave every quality number untouched while opening an injection path. A review that reports one axis tells you nothing about the other four, which is why the exam treats a single-number evaluation as incomplete by construction rather than merely weak.",
    "The axes are not equally easy to measure, and the hard ones are the ones teams postpone. Accuracy and latency fall out of any evaluation run, and cost falls out of the usage the API reports on every response. Safety and security need purpose-built adversarial sets: inputs designed to elicit harmful output, instructions hidden inside uploaded documents, requests for another customer's data. The belief that these can only be measured once production traffic exists is a stock distractor. They are measured before launch, on red-team cases, as harmful-output rate, injection success rate, data-leak rate and unauthorised-action rate.",
    { h: "Choosing the metric inside each axis" },
    "\"Accuracy\" is a family, not a metric. Exact match suits classification labels and extracted identifiers; F1 suits sets of extracted entities where partial credit matters; a rubric score suits open-ended text; groundedness, whether each claim is supported by the supplied context, suits retrieval-augmented answers. Pick the one that reflects what the business loses when the output is wrong. Latency works the same way: <b>TTFT</b> matters for a streaming chat interface where the user watches the first token arrive, while total time at p95 or p99 matters for a synchronous call another service is blocked on. A mean hides exactly the tail that breaches an SLA, which is why SLAs are written in percentiles.",
    "Cost belongs over outcomes, not requests. Cost per request flatters a cheaper configuration that fails more often and pushes work into retries or human review; cost per successfully completed task (see 1.6) exposes it. Model-stated confidence appears nowhere on this list because it is another output, not a measurement of correctness: the model can be confidently wrong. Confidence scores become useful only once calibrated against labelled data, and even then what you report is the measured accuracy in each confidence band, not the confidence itself.",
    { h: "Optimise one axis, constrain the rest" },
    "Five targets cannot all be maximised at once, so the architect names one objective, the axis tied to the stakeholder's pillar, and turns the other four into floors and ceilings. \"Maximise accuracy subject to p95 under 2 s, cost under the agreed ceiling per conversation, zero cross-customer disclosure and injection success below the agreed rate\" is a decision rule you can apply to every proposed change: a change that improves the objective but breaks any constraint is rejected, however large the gain. Safety and security constraints are usually zero-tolerance or close to it and are not traded against accuracy.",
    "Segmentation is how you check that the floors hold everywhere rather than on average. An aggregate of 97% can hide one document type failing four times in ten. Slicing by document type, tenant, intent, language and field turns a comfortable average into a list of failure pockets, and a pocket concentrated in one language or customer group is a fairness finding as well as a quality one (see 5.5).",
    { example: "A lender's review of a loan-document assistant reports 95% field accuracy on 800 documents against a 92% floor and asks for launch approval. The requirements also state p95 under 4 seconds, a cost ceiling per application, no exposure of one applicant's data to another, and support for scanned documents, which are a quarter of volume. The tempting answer is to approve because the accuracy floor is cleared. The right answer is to return the review as incomplete: it needs latency percentiles, cost per completed application, a disclosure metric and an injection-success metric from adversarial cases, each against its threshold, and accuracy segmented by document type, because the 95% could be averaging a strong digital-PDF score with a failing scanned one. The deciding detail is the list of stated requirements: each one without a measurement is a gap." }
  ],

  "4.2": [
    { h: "What lets a dataset catch a regression" },
    "An evaluation set exists to fail when the system gets worse, and that takes two properties that pull in different directions. <b>Representativeness</b>, a mix of document types, intents, languages and lengths that matches production, makes the score predict what users will experience. <b>Deliberate edge and adversarial cases</b>, the malformed input, the injected instruction, the question the corpus cannot answer, the failure you shipped last quarter, make it sensitive to breakages a representative sample rarely contains. A set drawn from one template or from the developer's favourite examples has neither.",
    "Independence is the third property, and the one teams lose without noticing. Every time you read a failing case and adjust the prompt to fix it, that case stops measuring generalisation and starts measuring memory. Keep a development set you iterate against and a held-out set you only score, and report the held-out number. Volume does not repair a contaminated or unrepresentative set: six hundred contracts from the same supplier's template give a tighter confidence interval around the wrong number.",
    "Labels are the other point of failure. Golden labels reviewed by domain experts are expensive, which is why the dataset is versioned like code: a score means something only relative to a named dataset version, and a relabelling is a change to be recorded like any prompt change.",
    { h: "The cheapest grader that can grade the criterion" },
    "Match the grader to the criterion, not to the task. Anything objective, such as schema validity, a required field, a prohibited phrase, an exact label or a passing unit test, goes to a <b>code-based grader</b>: fast, effectively free, deterministic and immune to drift. Because generation is non-deterministic, these checks assert properties of the output rather than an exact string. Qualitative criteria such as tone, completeness and faithfulness go to an <b>LLM judge with a written rubric</b>, because rubric-free judging is inconsistent and impossible to audit. <b>Humans</b> take the high-stakes, ambiguous and escalated samples, and they produce the labels the judge is calibrated against.",
    "Calibration is what turns a judge from an opinion into a measurement. Score a human-labelled sample with both; the disagreements are usually concentrated on one criterion, often completeness or subtle factual errors, and the fix is a sharper rubric line or a different judge. A judge that averages 4.8 while experts average 3.6 is telling you about the judge, and raising its pass threshold only applies a stricter cut to a biased score. The judge must also be independent of what it grades: a model rating its own output shares its blind spots, and so do test cases generated by the prompt under test.",
    { h: "From dataset to regression gate" },
    "The framework runs the full set on every change to the prompt, model, retrieval configuration or tools, in CI, with an explicit pass threshold on each guarded axis. Keep per-case results over time so you can see which case flipped, not just that the average moved; a flat average can hide ten fixes and ten new breakages. Starting small is fine, and 20 to 50 hand-written cases is enough to begin. The set grows from production: every failure found by monitoring (see 4.6) or reported by users becomes a labelled case, which is how the set keeps pace with drifting traffic instead of going stale.",
    { example: "An insurer's claims-summary generator writes 9,000 summaries a day for adjusters. The team proposes that the generating model score each summary from 1 to 10 and that anything under 7 goes to a person; the reported quality figure comes from the 40 claims used to write the prompt. The right framework has three layers: code checks on every summary for mandatory fields and prohibited statements, an independent judge with a rubric calibrated against an adjuster-labelled sample, and adjuster review of a stratified sample plus every escalation, all scored on a held-out set rebuilt from the production mix. Self-scoring fails twice, since it is neither independent nor calibrated, and the 40-claim figure is tuned data. The tempting alternative, a random 1% human sample alone, cannot scale the objective checks code runs on every output and will rarely land on a small failing segment." }
  ],

  "4.3": [
    { h: "Why the gates run in this order" },
    "Each gate is cheaper than the next and catches a class of failure the next would catch at greater cost. The regression set runs in minutes and says whether anything that used to work now breaks. Offline evaluation on the held-out set says whether the change is better on the objective and still inside every constraint. Only then does any user meet the change: a statistical A/B or a shadow test measures it on real traffic, a canary widens exposure to a small slice with rollback ready, and full rollout follows. Skipping a gate saves no time; it moves the discovery of a regression from a CI log to a customer.",
    "Versioning is the precondition for all of it. The prompt, the exact model identifier, the retrieval configuration and the tool definitions are versioned artefacts, and each arm of an experiment names one version of each. The previous version is not archived or deleted during a rollout, because it is the rollback target.",
    { h: "What makes an LLM A/B test valid" },
    "A comparison is valid only if exactly one thing differs between the arms. That is why a floating model alias is a confound even though both arms share it: a new release interacts differently with each prompt, so the baseline moves mid-test and the observed delta can no longer be attributed to the prompt. A re-index or a tool change landing during the test does the same. The remedy is to pin the model version and freeze the index for the duration, and to rerun if anything moved; extending the test does not separate the effect of the change from the effect of the disturbance.",
    "Randomise by user or session rather than by request, so one person does not see both variants within a conversation and contaminate the other arm's outcomes. Fix the metric and the sample size before starting and check significance before declaring a winner: a 0.3-point judge gain on twenty hand-picked examples is noise plus selection bias. Measure every guarded axis, not just the one the change targeted, since a longer, more thorough prompt that wins on quality often loses on output tokens, cost and p95.",
    { h: "Shadow, canary, and one variable at a time" },
    "Shadow mode runs the new variant on live inputs and stores its output without showing it to anyone. You get real-traffic measurements of quality, cost and latency with no user exposure, which makes shadow the right step for regulated, high-stakes or irreversible outputs, where even a small canary would put wrong output in front of customers. What shadow cannot measure is user behaviour, such as resolution, acceptance or satisfaction, so a change whose success is defined by user outcomes still needs a live A/B once shadow results are clean.",
    "Change one variable per iteration. If you swap the prompt and the model together and quality rises, you do not know which to keep; if it falls, you do not know which to revert. A changelog linking each version to its measured deltas lets a team understand months later why the system looks the way it does, and it is the record a governance review will ask for.",
    { example: "A bank tests dispute-letter prompt v12 against v11 in a two-week A/B. In the first week the retrieval team re-indexes the policy corpus, and v12 wins by four points on the judge score. The product owner wants to ship v12 and, in the same release, move to a newer model tier. The tempting reasoning is that both arms saw the same new index, so the comparison is fair. It is not: the re-index shifted the baseline mid-test and may have helped one prompt more than the other, so the four points cannot be attributed to v12. The architect reruns with the index frozen and the model pinned, ships v12 alone if it wins, and treats the model change as a separate iteration with its own regression run. Because dispute letters are regulated customer communications, each change goes through shadow before any canary." }
  ],

  "4.4": [
    { h: "Four failure classes, four signatures" },
    "Each failure class leaves a distinct pattern in the data, and the diagnosis is a matter of reading which pattern you have before touching anything. <b>Prompt failure</b> concerns instruction-following and form: the content is right but the structure varies, an instruction is ignored on a fraction of requests, or two readings of an ambiguous instruction both appear in the outputs. It does not depend on how hard the input is. <b>Hallucination</b>, more precisely grounding failure, is fabricated specifics such as a citation, a SKU or a figure, and it correlates with missing context: it concentrates where retrieval returned nothing relevant or the question falls outside the corpus. <b>Model mismatch</b> correlates with complexity: correct on simple inputs and wrong on multi-constraint ones, with a clear prompt and complete context. <b>Integration or data failure</b> correlates with time: a step change beginning at a deploy, a data refresh or a model release.",
    "Those correlations are the diagnostic tests. Split the failures by whether the context held the answer, by input complexity and by date, and the class usually falls out. The question to ask of any claimed hallucination is whether the right information was in the context. If it was not, the fix is retrieval coverage, citations constrained to retrieved passages and a permitted \"no source found\" outcome. If it was and the model ignored or misread it, you are looking at a prompt problem or a model mismatch instead.",
    "One sub-case deserves its own rule: correct values wrapped in prose, renamed keys or dropped optional fields is an output-handling failure. The durable fix is to enforce the structure with structured outputs or a forced tool schema and validate before use, not to upgrade the model or rewrite the paragraph describing the schema.",
    { h: "Finding the step where it went wrong" },
    "In a multi-step system the symptom surfaces several steps after its cause, so start from the trace (see 3.4) and find the first step whose output was already wrong. Replay that exact request and read the raw response. If the raw response is right and the application still misbehaves, the fault is in integration: parsing, validation or state handling. If the raw response is wrong, the fault is upstream in the prompt, the assembled context, the tool definitions or the model. Read <code>stop_reason</code> early, because <code>max_tokens</code> truncation looks like broken JSON and a refusal looks like a missing answer. Then compare the failing cases with the evaluation baseline to learn whether the problem is new or a pocket that was always there.",
    { h: "Why the obvious fix is usually wrong" },
    "The tempting fixes, a bigger model, a lower temperature, a longer prompt, are tempting because each is a one-line change. A bigger model does not retrieve documents the index never returned, and lower temperature narrows sampling without supplying missing facts or enforcing a schema. Every wrong attribution costs a full cycle of change, evaluate and roll out, and it can bury the real cause: a stronger model given the same empty context may simply fabricate more fluently. Exam stems plant the correlation in a clause such as \"model and latency unchanged\", \"since the document refresh\" or \"only when the search returned no results\", so find it before you read the options.",
    { example: "A pharmaceutical company's medical-information assistant has, since last Tuesday, been citing superseded dosing for one product line. The model version is pinned, latency is unchanged and the prompt has not changed in a month; last Tuesday the regulatory team replaced that product's label documents. The team proposes moving to the largest tier. The pattern is a step change in time aligned with a data refresh and confined to the refreshed content, so this is an integration and data failure, and retrieval and indexing come first: were the new labels ingested, were the old ones removed, did the new file format chunk correctly? The trace confirms retrieval is returning chunks of the old label. The model is faithfully summarising stale context, which is not a hallucination, and a larger model would repeat the stale dose just as faithfully." }
  ],

  "4.5": [
    { h: "Find where the money and the milliseconds go" },
    "Optimisation starts with a breakdown, not a lever. Each response's usage reports input, output, cache-creation and cache-read tokens separately; multiplied by rate and volume and attributed per feature and tenant, they show which term dominates. Output tokens are priced several times higher than input and are generated one at a time, so a verbose route is expensive in both money and time. A route with a long repeated prefix and short answers is a caching problem, and an agent that takes twelve sequential turns is a hop-count problem. Pulling a lever on a term that does not dominate is the classic wrong answer.",
    "Count everything in the cost: thinking tokens, cache writes, retries and the human review that failures trigger. Then express it per successfully completed task, because a cheaper configuration that fails more and pushes work into retries or escalation has raised the real cost.",
    { h: "The cost levers and when each one applies" },
    "Right-sizing the model per step usually pays most. A pipeline that runs classification, extraction and synthesis all on the largest tier is paying top-tier rates for steps a smaller tier handles within the floor. Demote one step at a time, rerun the evaluation each time, and keep the stronger tier for the hard tail. For routine routes, try a lower effort level on the same model before building a cascade to another one: it trades less and keeps one cache namespace. Drop extended thinking wherever the evaluation shows it buys no accuracy.",
    "Caching changes what the same request costs without trading any quality away, but only for a prefix that repeats exactly. Cache reads cost about a tenth of base input and writes carry a premium, so caching per-document content that never recurs pays the premium and collects nothing. A spend report dominated by cache writes with almost no reads means the prefix is unstable or not reused. Confirm caching from the cache-read tokens on the responses, not from the bill or from latency.",
    "The remaining levers are output length (an explicit length specification, a concise format, a tighter <code>max_tokens</code>), pruning tool results and history on long agent loops, fewer and better tools so every request carries less, and the Batch API for latency-tolerant work. Batch is a flat 50% discount with results returned within a 24-hour window, so it suits a nightly job and never a request a user is waiting on.",
    { h: "Latency is a different problem" },
    "Latency levers only partly overlap with cost ones. Streaming cuts neither total time nor cost but transforms time to first token, which is what an interactive user perceives. Caching reduces TTFT on long prefixes, and a smaller model or a shorter output cuts generation time. For agents the large wins are structural: parallel tool calls instead of sequential ones, fewer hops, no reasoning the step does not need. Tail latency is fixed where the tail originates. Find the rare path in the traces, such as a retrieval fan-out or a retry storm, then cap it, parallelise it or give it a fast fallback; a global change applied to every request is the wrong instrument for a p99 breach.",
    "Every lever is a hypothesis tested against the evaluation set. A saving that takes quality below the agreed floor is a regression and is rejected like any other, which is also why optimisation follows, and never precedes, showing that the task is solved at all.",
    { example: "A legal-tech firm reviews 20,000 contracts nightly for reports read the next morning. Every step, clause classification, field extraction and risk summary, runs synchronously on the largest tier with extended thinking, and each contract's full text is marked for caching. The spend report shows output tokens as the largest share, from long risk summaries, and heavy cache writes with almost no reads. The team proposes caching more aggressively and streaming every call. The right moves are to submit the job through the Batch API, run classification and extraction on a smaller tier validated against the floor, drop thinking where the evaluation shows no gain, constrain the length of the summaries, and cache only the shared instruction prefix. Streaming is wrong because nobody is waiting and it saves nothing; caching contract text is wrong because no contract is ever sent twice." }
  ],

  "4.6": [
    { h: "What every log record must let you reconstruct" },
    "The test for production telemetry is whether, months later, you can reconstruct why a specific run produced a specific output. That needs the versions, the prompt version and the exact model ID, because without them you cannot tell whether a change in behaviour came from your own deploy or from the model underneath. It needs usage broken out into cache reads and writes, latency, and <code>stop_reason</code>, because truncation and refusals hide inside successful responses. It needs every tool call with its arguments, latency and outcome, tied together into a trace by a correlation ID (see 3.4). Metadata is kept for every request; full payloads are sampled.",
    "Silent failures are the reason all of this matters. A truncated answer and a refusal both arrive as successful responses, and a server-side tool that fails reports its error inside a successful response body, so monitoring that counts exceptions never sees it. Alert on the fields that carry these signals, not only on exceptions and 5xx.",
    { h: "Availability metrics cannot see quality" },
    "The error rate, CPU and p95 can all stay flat while answers get worse: after a data refresh, as users start asking questions the prompt was never tuned for, or when a floating model alias moves. Quality needs its own signals. Score a sample of production outputs with the same judge and rubric the evaluation set uses, so that production and offline numbers are comparable. Track user feedback such as thumbs-down and rephrased questions; the rates of fallback, \"unknown\", refusal and human escalation; retrieval hit quality; and drift in the input distribution, which warns that the evaluation set is going stale before any score falls. Trend these weekly and alert on movement, not only on fixed thresholds.",
    "Dashboards and alerts on SLO breaches, latency percentiles and cost per feature or tenant complete the picture. A sudden cost jump is often the first visible sign of a looping agent or a broken cache, well before anyone complains.",
    { h: "Privacy applies to telemetry" },
    "Logs are a data store, and HIPAA and GDPR govern them as they govern any other. Redact or pseudonymise before writing, not afterwards; restrict access to raw payloads to the people whose job needs them; set retention to what diagnosis requires. The tension between diagnosability and minimisation is resolved by keeping full metadata on every request and redacted, sampled payloads, not by writing everything to a shared platform and keeping it indefinitely.",
    { h: "Closing the loop" },
    "Monitoring earns its keep when it feeds evaluation. A failure found in production becomes a labelled case in the evaluation set, so the regression gate (see 4.2 and 4.3) blocks any future change that brings it back. Without that step, the same failure is fixed, forgotten and shipped again with the next prompt revision.",
    { example: "A hospital's patient-portal assistant has green dashboards: 0.2% errors, stable p95 and flat cost. Nurses report that it increasingly tells patients to \"contact your provider\" for questions it used to answer. Its logs write full prompts, patient details included, to the organisation-wide log platform with two years' retention. Asked what to change, the tempting answer is more infrastructure alerting, such as p99 and CPU, but those measure availability and were already green. The right answer adds quality signals: judge scores on sampled outputs and a weekly trend of the fallback rate, which would have shown the rise weeks earlier. It fixes the telemetry itself by redacting patient data before logging, restricting payload access and cutting retention to what diagnosis needs. The failing conversations become evaluation cases, and diagnosing the cause of the rise is a separate step (see 4.4)." }
  ],

  "5.1": [
    { h: "Why one layer is never enough" },
    "Every layer in the stack catches a different class of failure, and every layer has a known blind spot. An input filter sees only what the user sends, so it misses the indirect injection that arrives later inside a retrieved document or a tool result. A system-prompt policy shapes behaviour but only asks. Output validation catches a malformed or ungrounded answer, but only after generation, and it cannot recall a tool call that has already run. Programmatic tool controls enforce at the action itself, but only on the actions you thought to wrap, and monitoring catches the rest only after the fact.",
    "That is what the lesson means by each layer covering the others' failures, and it is why the layers must be independent: one bypass should not take out the whole stack. The exam tests this in reverse. A statement that an output classifier makes input filtering unnecessary, or that a single strict input filter is sufficient, is the false one — each layer addresses failures the others cannot see.",
    { h: "Reading the strength hierarchy" },
    "The hierarchy ranks controls by what they guarantee. Removing a capability is strongest because nothing is left to misuse: if the summarisation path has no email tool, no injected instruction can send an email. Blocking programmatically — an allowlist, a hook that refuses a destructive command, a hard limit inside the tool — is deterministic, though only as good as the rule. An approval gate compensates by making the action wait for a person, a prompt instruction merely requests, and logging detects without preventing anything. The classification items use exactly this split: removal and blocks are preventive, approval gates compensating, logs and after-generation classifiers detective.",
    "The decision rule is to pick the highest rung the role allows. Remove what the role never needs, block what it needs only within limits, gate what needs judgement each time, and treat prompt wording and logs as supporting layers. When a stem says <i>must never</i> or <i>regardless of what the user asks</i>, the answer names a mechanism, never a sentence in the system prompt. Hence the trap of a confirmation prompt on a capability the role never uses: it leaves the capability exposed and adds reviewer load for nothing.",
    { h: "Proportionality: scaling to blast radius" },
    "Blast radius is what the worst plausible action can do. A read-only assistant over public documentation still gets the layered basics — input filtering, a scoped system prompt, output validation, monitoring and least-privilege read-only tools — because injection, leakage and wrong answers still happen. A treasury agent that moves funds adds action-layer limits, idempotency checks, human approval before execution and a kill switch. Applying the treasury stack everywhere for consistency is wrong: per-response approval on a read-only tool costs money and breeds fatigue without reducing risk. So is the opposite answer, no controls because it is read-only.",
    "Blast radius changes when capabilities change, and the stack must change before the capability is enabled. A read-only assistant that learns to send external email needs recipient allowlists or limits, approval for external recipients, idempotency and an audit of every send in place first. A red-team schedule, a dashboard or a prompt asking the model to double-check recipients are supporting measures, not the precondition.",
    { h: "Guardrails you can measure" },
    "An untested guardrail is an assumption. Put adversarial and safety cases into the evaluation suite (see 4.2) — injection attempts through every untrusted input channel, requests that should be refused and borderline ones that should be allowed — and track bypass rate under the security axis of 4.1. Re-run them on every prompt, tool or model change, because a control that held on one version can fail silently on the next.",
    { example: "A procurement agent pays supplier invoices. Its controls are a system prompt capping payments without a note, an output classifier that flags suspicious text, and full action logging. In a test, an instruction hidden in a supplier PDF causes a payment far above the cap; the classifier flags it after execution and the logs record it. The tempting responses are to add injection examples to the prompt or to turn logging into real-time alerting so operations can reverse payments quickly. Both stay on the request and detect rungs — the next rephrased injection gets through, and a reversal is not prevention. The correct conclusion is that the stack has no enforcement at the action: the payment tool needs a programmatic limit, an idempotency check and a human approval gate above the threshold before execution, and invoice content must be treated as untrusted data that can never authorise a payment." }
  ],

  "5.2": [
    { h: "From a list to a risk register" },
    "The failure modes in the points sort into four families, and each family points to a different kind of control. <b>Content</b> failures — hallucination, degraded behaviour on out-of-distribution inputs, context drift in long sessions — mean the output is wrong. <b>Adversarial</b> failures — prompt injection through user or retrieved content, jailbreaks — mean someone else is steering the model. <b>Operational</b> failures — non-determinism, tool mis-selection, silent behaviour change across model versions, runaway loops that blow up cost — mean the system no longer behaves as it did when you tested it. <b>Human</b> failure — over-reliance — means users stop checking. This is why \"add guardrails\" is never a complete answer: grounding checks do nothing for runaway loops, and iteration caps do nothing for hallucination.",
    "Scoring each mode on likelihood, impact and detectability turns the list into a register. Detectability is the factor teams forget and the one that matters most here. A 500 error is loud; a fluent, confident, wrong answer is silent. A frequent, low-impact failure that is easy to catch — malformed JSON rejected by a schema validator — needs far less than a rare, high-impact, hard-to-detect one such as an injected instruction triggering an outbound action. Each row gets a control and a named owner, because a risk nobody owns is a risk nobody notices changing.",
    { h: "The false beliefs the exam plants" },
    "Many distractors are false assumptions about the model, and the true-or-false items test them directly. A larger model tier does not remove injection risk; a model that follows instructions better may follow injected ones better too. Temperature 0 does not guarantee byte-identical output. A model update behind a floating alias can silently change output format and refusal behaviour, which is why production pins the version and migrates through regression evaluations (see 2.1 and 4.3). A provider's published certification does not make your system compliant (see 5.4).",
    "Confidence is the subtlest of these. A confident tone is not evidence, and the model's own confidence rating is poorly calibrated — the CCAR-F guide lists self-rated confidence as an unreliable escalation trigger, because the model can be confidently wrong. That is the reason honest communication of limitations belongs in the design: outputs are probabilistic, accuracy is never guaranteed at 100 %, knowledge has a cutoff and results are sensitive to phrasing. Over-reliance is the human consequence of hiding these facts, and it is mitigated architecturally — citations a user can check, disclosure that AI produced the output, and a visible route to a person — not by a line in the terms of use.",
    { h: "Graceful degradation" },
    "Decide in advance what the system does when a failure fires. The options are a fallback (a rule-based path, the previous pinned model), a decline (\"I cannot answer that from the sources available\"), an escalation to a human with a self-contained summary, or a rollback to the last known-good version. Runaway loops need an iteration or spend cap enforced in code rather than requested in the prompt. The design question is never whether the system can fail — it can — but whether each failure is contained, visible and owned when it happens.",
    { example: "A law firm's research assistant retrieves from internal case files and public web pages, has no write tools, and lawyers paste its answers into client memos. The partner asks for the top risks and what to do. The tempting answer is to move to the largest model tier to cut both hallucination and injection. It addresses neither reliably: size does not remove injection, and a stronger model can still fabricate a citation fluently. Reason through the register instead. Fabricated citations are high-impact and hard to detect, so they get mandatory citations to retrieved sources, a groundedness check and lawyer review before anything leaves the firm. Injection through web pages is likely but its impact is bounded because the path has no write or send tools — keep it that way, and treat fetched content as data. Over-reliance is the quiet risk: disclose AI involvement and make the sources visible so checking is easy. Each control gets an owner in the register." }
  ],

  "5.3": [
    { h: "Where the gate goes" },
    "The four placements in the points share one test: the cost of an undetected error is high or cannot be undone. <b>Irreversible</b> actions — a payment sent, a record deleted, an email delivered — cannot be caught downstream, so the check has to come before execution. <b>Regulated</b> decisions in credit, health or employment carry a requirement for human sign-off and for a record an auditor can reconstruct. <b>Low-confidence</b> or <b>high-impact</b> outputs are where the model is least reliable or the stakes are highest. <b>Sampled</b> routine outputs are different in kind: they are not a gate but a measurement, telling you whether the auto-approved tier is still safe to leave unreviewed.",
    "Confidence as a routing signal needs care. The CCAR-F guide warns that a model's self-rated confidence is poorly calibrated. If you route on confidence, use field-level scores calibrated against a labelled validation set, and keep stratified random sampling on the high-confidence stream so new error patterns surface. Validate by segment before automating: the guide's example is a 97 % overall accuracy hiding a 40 % error rate on one document type. Route on risk tier as well as confidence, so a confident output on an irreversible action still reaches a person.",
    { h: "Making the gate architectural" },
    "\"The action does not execute without approval\" means the approval is a precondition checked in code. The tool that closes an account refuses to run without an approval record; a pre-tool-use hook redirects an over-threshold refund to an approval queue; the workflow cannot advance past the gate state. A system prompt telling the model to defer to humans, or to remind the officer that the decision is theirs, enforces nothing. Neither does showing a draft on the officer's screen: if the system can finalise without the officer's action, there is no gate.",
    "Then design what the reviewer sees and what gets stored. The reviewer needs the recommendation, the evidence or citations behind it and its confidence — enough to disagree in an informed way. When a case is escalated, the reviewer may see only the handoff, so the summary must stand on its own: who, what was tried, what is recommended and why it was escalated. Store the model's recommendation and the human decision as <b>separate provenance fields</b> with timestamps. If you keep only the final decision, an auditor cannot tell an override from a rubber stamp, and you cannot measure reviewer agreement, which is the number that tells you whether the model is improving.",
    { h: "Reviewer fatigue is a control failure" },
    "Reviewing everything feels safest and is the trap. The symptoms are recognisable in a stem: approval rates near 100 %, review time collapsing to a few seconds, harmful items approved anyway. At that point the gate exists only on paper. A second reviewer doubles the cost and the fatigue; replacing reviewers with a classifier removes the human from exactly the decisions that need one. The fix is routing: auto-approve low-risk, high-confidence items; send irreversible, regulated and low-confidence items to review with evidence shown; sample the rest for QA; batch low-risk work; and track agreement and turnaround so the thresholds can be tuned.",
    { example: "A telecom's service agent can issue account credits and close accounts. Policy says small credits may be automatic, closures need a supervisor, and auditors must see what the agent proposed. Today the system prompt says \"ask a supervisor before closing an account\", every credit also goes to the supervisor queue to be safe, and the database stores only final account state. Supervisors now approve almost everything in seconds. The tempting fix is a firmer prompt instruction about closures. It still enforces nothing, and it leaves the fatigue untouched. The right redesign puts the gate in the close-account tool so it cannot run without an approval record, lets credits under the threshold execute with sampled QA, shows supervisors the evidence and confidence for each closure, and stores the proposal and the supervisor's decision as separate timestamped fields." }
  ],

  "5.4": [
    { h: "Identify the regime first" },
    "A compliance stem usually turns on which regime is triggered, so read for the trigger before reading the options. <b>GDPR</b> is triggered by the personal data of people in the EU, wherever it sits — prompts, embeddings, logs. <b>HIPAA</b> is triggered by US protected health information, and the stem often signals it with a third party processing that data. <b>FedRAMP</b> is triggered by a US federal agency workload in the cloud. One system can trigger more than one regime, and the controls stack.",
    "The regimes ask different questions. GDPR asks why you are processing the data, how little you can use, how long you keep it, and whether the person can see or erase it. HIPAA asks whether you use only the minimum necessary, whether each processor is under a BAA, and who can access the data with what audit trail. FedRAMP asks whether the environment you deploy into is authorised at the level the workload requires.",
    { h: "GDPR: the obligation follows every copy" },
    "Purpose limitation, data minimisation and storage limitation all attach to every store the data reaches, and so do data-subject rights. An erasure request is not satisfied by deleting the conversation transcript while embeddings of the customer's emails sit in the retrieval index and two years of prompts sit in the logs. Embeddings are derived personal data and can be deleted, so \"erasure is technically impossible\" is a distractor — as is hosting the index in an EU region under a data-processing agreement, which answers a residency question, not an erasure one. What makes deletion complete is a <b>documented data flow</b>: you can only delete from every store if you know every store.",
    "The rest of the GDPR list still applies to the design: high-risk processing calls for a DPIA, transfer rules govern where the data may go, and residency requirements are met through EU regions or the Bedrock, Vertex and Foundry options (see 2.1).",
    { h: "HIPAA: minimum necessary, before the model and before the log" },
    "Minimum necessary is a design constraint on the request itself: send only the fields the task needs, de-identified or redacted before the model call, under a BAA with the processor. Logs are where most designs fail. Full prompts containing diagnoses and member identifiers written to a shared analytics platform are PHI in an uncontrolled store. Redact at the point of writing, restrict access to authorised roles, set retention limits and keep an audit trail of access. Disabling logging entirely looks safe and is wrong, because it removes the audit trail the obligations require. A bigger model tier changes nothing about what data you may send.",
    { h: "FedRAMP: a precondition, not a control" },
    "FedRAMP works as a gate on where you may deploy: only in environments authorised at the required impact level, which restricts the acceptable endpoints and regions. It appears in sequencing items. After threat modelling and guardrail design, the step that must be complete before a federal deployment is confirming that both the hosting environment and the model endpoint hold that authorisation. Good guardrails, published limitations or a scheduled red team do not substitute for it.",
    { h: "The shared control set and the provider boundary" },
    "The recurring controls — redaction or pseudonymisation before the model, tenant isolation, audit logging, retention policies, deletion workflows, documented data flows, vendor agreements — are worth building once, because every regime draws on them. The boundary to remember is that the provider's compliance covers the provider's processing, not your stores. Prompts you keep, traces, error messages, evaluation sets and vector indexes all accumulate copies, and logs are usually the least protected. Regulated values also do not belong in tool schema definitions, which are handled separately from message content.",
    { example: "A US clinic group wants Claude to draft appointment follow-up letters. The pipeline sends each patient's full chart, including diagnoses and identifiers, and writes every prompt and response to an observability platform most of engineering can read. The team argues that the model provider's compliance covers the pipeline. It does not: the provider's position says nothing about your request payloads or your logs. The tempting alternative, switching logging off, trades one failure for another by destroying the audit trail. The right answer has two parts. Put a BAA in place with the processor and send only the minimum necessary, de-identified fields the letter needs. Then redact PHI before anything is logged, restrict log access to authorised roles, set a retention limit and audit who reads the logs." }
  ],

  "5.5": [
    { h: "Why aggregate accuracy hides unfairness" },
    "An aggregate figure averages away the groups where the system fails. A screening tool that agrees with past recruiter decisions 91 % of the time may be faithfully reproducing whatever bias those decisions contained — agreement with a biased label is not fairness, and a larger evaluation set drawn from the same decisions only narrows the confidence interval on a number that measures the wrong thing. The CCAR-F guide makes the same point about accuracy by document type: 97 % overall can hide a 40 % error rate in one segment. Fairness is assessed on <b>segmented</b> outcomes across protected or otherwise relevant groups.",
    "Bias can enter without a protected attribute ever appearing. Proxy variables in the prompt or in retrieved context — a postcode, a school name, a gap in employment history — can stand in for one. The practical check is to list every field the prompt and the retrieval step feed to the model and ask whether the task needs it. Removing unneeded fields is data minimisation (see 5.4) doing double duty as a fairness control. Keep humans in consequential decisions (see 5.3), so a skewed recommendation is a draft someone reviews rather than an outcome.",
    { h: "Transparency has two audiences" },
    "For the people affected, transparency means disclosing that AI is involved, giving an explanation or citations for the output, and offering a route to a human. For the organisation, it means documenting model behaviour and limitations in model or system cards and decision records, so the next team knows what the system was built to do and where it is weak. The distractors invert these: present outputs as human-authored so customers trust them, hide the model's role to avoid alarming users, or remove confidence indicators so nobody is confused. Each one increases over-reliance, which 5.2 treats as a failure mode in its own right.",
    { h: "Accountability outlives the launch" },
    "Accountability is what keeps the other two working after go-live: a named owner for the system and for each guardrail, audit trails, an incident process and a periodic review of guardrails and evaluations. Without an owner, the segmented evaluation that passed before launch is never re-run when the model, the prompt or the applicant population changes.",
    { h: "A design input, not a checklist" },
    "Each of these has architectural consequences, which is why it has to be decided during design. Segmented evaluation needs an evaluation set built so outcomes can be sliced by the relevant groups (see 4.2). Disclosure and explanation need space in the interface and a retrieval path that keeps its citations. Recourse needs an escalation route with people behind it. Retrofitting any of these after complaints is expensive, and the exam treats a post-launch fairness audit as an action from the wrong phase.",
    { example: "A university wants a tool that summarises applications and ranks them for admissions staff. The vendor reports 93 % agreement with the last five years of admissions decisions and calls the tool fair. Before launch, what is required? The tempting answer is a larger validation set drawn from more historical decisions, which sounds rigorous but only measures agreement with the past more precisely. The required elements are outcome analysis segmented by the relevant applicant groups, a review of which inputs could act as proxies, admissions staff making the actual decision, disclosure to applicants with an explanation and a route to human review, and documented limitations with a named owner who re-runs the segmented evaluation on a schedule." }
  ],

  "6.1": [
    { h: "What discovery is for" },
    "Discovery exists because every later decision needs an input that only the business can supply. The success metric becomes the evaluation set and the quality SLO. The constraints decide the model tier, the deployment target and which controls are mandatory. The current workflow gives you the baseline you will be measured against, the failure cost tells you where human gates belong, and the decision owner is the person who approves scope changes later. Skip any one of these and the design still gets made — just on an assumption nobody wrote down.",
    "That is why the output is a <i>written and agreed</i> document rather than a shared understanding. The agreement is the exit criterion. A problem statement the sponsor has not signed off will be reinterpreted the first time results disappoint, and a success metric that exists only in the architect's head cannot be used to settle an argument.",
    "One useful discipline is to sort each clause of the brief into functional requirements (what the system must do: inputs, outputs, accuracy, languages, systems it reads and writes) and infrastructure requirements (how it must run: latency, availability, residency, identity, cost ceiling). \"It must work for our EU customers\" sounds like a feature but is a residency constraint that can decide where the model is called from.",
    { h: "Who you talk to changes what you learn" },
    "Each interview group surfaces a different class of fact. The sponsor gives you the why and the budget. The people who do the work give you where time is actually lost, the edge cases, and the gap between the documented process and the real one — which is why workflow shadowing and a review of sample data matter: they show the true distribution of inputs rather than the one described in a meeting. Security and compliance give you the constraints that can veto a design, and they are cheapest to learn before anything is built.",
    "Explicit non-goals and must/should/could prioritisation are what make discovery useful later. When the deadline tightens, the prioritised list tells you what to drop; when someone asks for a new capability mid-build, the non-goals tell you it was deliberately excluded and has to come through change control (see 6.3).",
    { h: "Deadlines and proofs of concept" },
    "A hard deadline is an argument for time-boxing discovery, not skipping it. The deadline should shape the scope of the first release, not the process that decides what the release is. Likewise a PoC is legitimate discovery only when the open question is technical feasibility — can the model extract these fields from these scanned forms at a usable rate — and it needs exit criteria written in advance, so that it answers a question instead of quietly becoming the product. A PoC started before success criteria exist answers \"can we build something\", which nobody was asking.",
    { h: "How the exam frames it" },
    "Stems pile on urgency (\"competitors have it\", \"by Q1\", \"start building\") and then drop the detail that decides the item: nobody has measured the current process, compliance has not been consulted, the metric is \"reduce workload\". Any of those means discovery is incomplete, and the right option is the one that completes it proportionately. The distractors look like progress — a PoC, a model choice, a system prompt draft — and all of them are design actions taken without a target. See 1.1 for the prior question of whether a language model should do the task at all.",
    { example: "The head of claims at an insurer wants \"AI to speed up claims triage\" before year end. There are 2,000 historical claims available, legal has not been asked about handling medical documents, and a handler mentions in passing that most delay comes from waiting for missing documents, not from reading them. Options: build a triage-classification PoC on the historical claims; choose the model tier so procurement can start; run a time-boxed discovery with handlers, legal and the sponsor to fix the metric, the baseline and the constraints, then scope a first release; draft the triage prompt. The handler's remark is the deciding detail: it suggests the bottleneck is a chasing workflow, not classification, so a classification PoC could hit its accuracy target and move the business metric not at all. The tempting answer is the PoC, because the data exists — but available data tells you what you <i>can</i> build, not what the problem is. Time-boxed discovery is correct, and it also brings legal in before medical data flows anywhere." }
  ],

  "6.2": [
    { h: "A decision is a trade-off, not a verdict" },
    "Stakeholders do not need to be told what you chose; they need to know what they are getting and what it costs them. The four questions in the lesson — what we gain, what we give up, why now, what would change the decision — turn a design choice into something a stakeholder can accept or challenge on its merits. Anchor the framing on the pillar that stakeholder named (see 1.6): if the sponsor's goal is cost, the decision is presented as a cost trade-off, with quality and latency held as constraints.",
    "The fourth question is the one most often left out and the one that does the most work. Naming the evidence that would reopen a decision — traffic tripling, a new document type failing the quality floor, a regulation changing where data may be processed — converts a future argument into a future measurement. It also protects the decision: when a successor wants to reverse it, the test for doing so is already written down.",
    { h: "Architecture Decision Records" },
    "An ADR runs in a fixed order: context, the options considered, the decision, its consequences. The order matters. Context first, because a decision only makes sense against the constraints that existed when it was made. Options second, with their costs, because a record that lists only the winner invites the next team to rediscover and re-propose the alternatives you already rejected. Consequences last, including the negative ones, because they tell a reader what they have inherited.",
    "The ADR lives in the repository, next to the code it explains, so it is versioned, reviewable and found by the people who change the system. The alternatives the exam offers all fail on one property: a meeting recording is not searchable or reviewable, a diagram shows what was built but not why, and a pinned model ID in configuration shows what is running but nothing about the reasoning.",
    { h: "One decision, three audiences" },
    "The same decision is told at different depths: executives get outcome, risk and cost; engineers get contracts and constraints; compliance gets data flows and controls. Tailoring is not simplification — each audience needs complete information about the thing it is accountable for, and nothing it has to translate. When an executive has explicitly said they do not want token detail, the professional move is to translate: cost per case, response time, whether the agreed quality floor is met. Preparing these framings is work done <i>before</i> a joint workshop, not improvised in it.",
    "Probabilistic behaviour needs its own translation. \"It is accurate\" and \"it scores 94%\" are both incomplete. The committable statement names the evaluation set, gives the measured result with a range, says what happens when the system is wrong (escalation, human review, fallback), and says which dimensions the SLA actually covers (see 6.3).",
    { example: "In an architecture review, a bank's CISO asks: \"Could this assistant show one business customer another customer's documents?\" The architect can explain how the embedding model represents documents; say that Claude is trained to refuse such requests; walk through the data flow — retrieval filtered by the caller's tenant before anything reaches the model, the tools scoped to the user's permissions — point to the ADR that rejected a shared unfiltered index and why, and state the residual risk and how it is detected; or show the benchmark accuracy of the retrieval step. The right answer is the data-flow walk-through with the controls, the rejected alternative and the residual risk: it answers the risk question in the CISO's terms. The embedding explanation is the tempting wrong answer because it is accurate and technical, but it is engineering depth delivered to the wrong audience and does not address isolation. The refusal claim is not a control at all, and retrieval accuracy measures a different property." }
  ],

  "6.3": [
    { h: "Why a correctness guarantee cannot be signed" },
    "A contractual accuracy figure is a promise about how the model will behave on inputs nobody has seen yet. The architecture does not control that; it can only measure it. So the professional response to \"99.9% correct\" is neither yes nor a flat no — it is a restructuring of the commitment into parts the system can honour. Refusing any SLA is as wrong as accepting the absolute: the client has a legitimate need for enforceable terms, and the job is to supply terms that are real.",
    "The variants the exam offers are worth recognising. \"Promise 100% with human review of every answer\" turns the system into a human process with a model attached, destroys the economics that justified it, and still is not a guarantee. \"Accept it with a renegotiation clause\" signs something known to be false. An SLA on tokens or cost per conversation is measurable and enforceable but commits to nothing about quality, which is what was asked.",
    { h: "SLAs and SLOs are different instruments" },
    "Split the commitment by who controls the dimension. Availability, latency and the human response time on escalations are engineering-controlled, so they can be SLAs in the ordinary sense. Quality is measured, so it becomes an SLO: a threshold on an agreed, <i>versioned</i> evaluation set, measured on sampled production traffic at a stated cadence, with a defined review and escalation when it breaches. Versioning is not a detail — if the evaluation set changes, the number changes meaning, and a threshold on an unversioned set can be met or missed by editing the set.",
    "Each commitment also needs its breach path written down: how a breach is detected, who is told, how quickly, and what happens next — rollback to the previous prompt or model version, routing to humans, a root-cause review. A threshold with no detection mechanism is a hope rather than an SLO.",
    { h: "Feedback loops that produce evidence" },
    "Expectation alignment is continuous, not a launch event. Regular demos on real data keep the stakeholder's picture tied to the actual distribution. A pilot with a defined cohort makes results attributable and comparable. Feedback channels are designed so that each complaint becomes an evaluation case and, where it is a new need, a scoped change request (see 4.2 for datasets). Change control assesses each request against the agreed success criteria and constraints, not against whoever asked most recently.",
    "The single most damaging habit is showing the best example. A weekly highlight reel trains the sponsor to expect the tail of the distribution; when rollout targets are then set from that impression, the gap between expectation and measured performance becomes a credibility problem rather than an engineering one. Show the distribution: the share that passes, the categories of failure, and how each is handled.",
    { example: "A pilot HR-policy assistant has been demoed weekly with standout answers. The sponsor now wants to add payroll questions and write a 98% accuracy target into the company-wide rollout agreement. The versioned evaluation set of 400 cases shows 86%, with failures concentrated on questions that span two policies. The right response has three parts: present the distribution and failure categories so the sponsor sees where 86% comes from; propose a quality SLO at the measured level on the versioned set, sampled in production, with a breach process and an improvement plan targeted at multi-policy questions, alongside availability and latency SLAs; and treat payroll as a change request, with its own evaluation cases, assessed against the agreed criteria. The tempting wrong answer is accepting 98% backed by human review of disputed answers — it feels responsive, but it is still a promise the architecture cannot keep, and it moves the cost onto reviewers without anyone having agreed to it." }
  ],

  "6.4": [
    { h: "Write for the reader you will never meet" },
    "The test for a handoff package is whether a competent team, with no access to you, could build, operate and safely change the system from it. That team will read it at inconvenient moments — during an incident, before a model upgrade, when a regulator asks how data flows — and every ambiguity becomes either a phone call to someone who has left or a guess. So the documentation is precise (a schema, not \"roughly the customer object\"), versioned, and kept in the repository next to the code, so that a change to one shows up in review alongside the other.",
    { h: "What each artefact lets someone do" },
    "It helps to read the handoff list as a list of jobs rather than documents. Diagrams and data flows let someone assess the impact of a change and answer a compliance question. Component contracts — schemas, APIs, tool definitions — let them modify one part without breaking another. Prompt and model versions tell them exactly what is running and what to roll back to. Evaluation datasets and thresholds let them decide whether a change is safe to ship. Controls documentation keeps the compliance posture intact. Runbooks let on-call respond to alerts and roll back. The cost model lets them spot an anomaly, known limitations stop them over-promising, and ADRs tell them why (see 6.2).",
    "Seen that way, the common inadequate packages fail for clear reasons. A slide deck cannot be executed, cannot be versioned against the code and almost never contains thresholds. Source code alone shows what the system does but not the intent, the quality floor, the rollback path or the limits. A recorded demo shows the happy path once. Vendor comparison lists and working notes are useful history, but they do not help anyone run the system.",
    { h: "Implementation guidance and change boundaries" },
    "Implementation guidance tells the receiving team what to build first, how to verify each component before moving on, and — most importantly — what must not change without re-evaluation: prompts, model IDs and retrieval configuration. These are singled out because they change behaviour without changing the shape of the code. A retrieval chunk size or a model identifier looks like configuration, passes every type check and unit test, and can move quality by a wide margin. A model release is a dependency upgrade: it gets a branch, a regression run against the thresholds, and a deliberate rollout. Stating this boundary explicitly, with the evaluation gate that enforces it, is what lets another team change the system without degrading it.",
    "This is also why the lesson says operational documentation is part of the architecture. An SLA is a promise that someone can detect a breach and respond within a time limit; without runbooks and thresholds, nobody can do either, so the SLA exists only on paper.",
    { example: "A fraud-alert summariser is handed to a platform team with architecture documents, prompts in the repository and a thorough README. Six weeks later the platform team increases the retrieval chunk size to reduce latency. Nothing errors, but analysts start complaining that summaries omit transaction details, and nobody notices for a month. Which handoff omission caused this? Options: more onboarding sessions for the platform team; a more detailed architecture diagram; stating retrieval configuration as a change boundary, with the evaluation dataset and thresholds that gate it, and a quality signal in monitoring; restricting repository access to the original team. The third is correct: the change was reasonable on its face, and only a documented boundary plus an evaluation gate would have caught its quality cost. Training is the tempting answer, but no amount of onboarding tells a team that a latency tweak needs a quality re-run unless the documentation says so, and restricting access defeats the purpose of a handoff." }
  ],

  "6.5": [
    { h: "Phases defined by what they hand on" },
    "The lifecycle is easiest to reason about as a sequence of exit criteria. Discovery ends when the problem, metrics and constraints are written and agreed. Design ends when the pattern, models, integration, evaluation plan and controls are chosen and recorded in ADRs. Build and validate end when evaluation gates and the security review pass. Handoff ends when the operating team can run the system without you — documentation, training, runbooks. Monitoring is in place when telemetry, sampled quality measurement and cost tracking are live. Iteration is healthy when monitoring and feedback reach a named owner with a change process.",
    "Each phase produces an artefact the next one consumes, which is why a skipped phase shows up later as rework rather than disappearing. A missing success metric is not noticed in discovery; it surfaces weeks into the build as an argument about whether an answer is good.",
    { h: "Diagnosing a symptom by phase" },
    "The recurring item type gives you a symptom and asks where it should have been caught. The key move is to separate when the symptom appeared from where the missing artefact should have been produced. A team that cannot agree what a good answer looks like three weeks into the build has a discovery gap. Uncertainty about whether a mid-tier model meets the floor on complex cases is a design and evaluation question. On-call not knowing which prompt version was live is a handoff and monitoring gap. Regressions found only through customer complaints mean monitoring lacks a quality signal. Quality decaying over months with nobody owning prompts or the index is an iteration failure.",
    "The trap in the lesson — fixing a discovery gap with a design action — follows from this. A bigger model or a new framework cannot supply a success criterion nobody defined; it just produces different outputs that still cannot be judged. Equally, decay after a long period in production is not usually a discovery failure: the requirements may have been fine, and what is missing is the owner and the loop.",
    { h: "Iteration is budgeted, not optional" },
    "Three forces make iteration mandatory. Traffic drifts away from the distribution the evaluation set was built on. Regulations change what data may be processed and how. And models move through a lifecycle — Active, Legacy, Deprecated, Retired — with Anthropic giving at least 60 days' notice to customers with active deployments of a publicly released model before retirement, after which requests naming it fail. Partner clouds publish their own schedules, so the same model can be on different dates in different environments. A system with no owner and no current evaluation run cannot respond to any of these in time, so iteration needs a budget and a name against it from the start.",
    { example: "Three months after launch, contact-centre supervisors complain that call summaries often miss the refund amount agreed with the customer. The team proposes moving to a larger model. On investigation, the original success criterion was \"summaries are useful to supervisors\", and the evaluation set scores overall readability; no field was ever defined as required. Which phase should have caught this? Discovery: the missing artefact is a measurable criterion — which fields a summary must contain — agreed with supervisors, and the fix starts there, followed by evaluation cases for those fields. The larger model is the tempting wrong answer: it is a design-phase action, and without a defined criterion the team cannot even show it helped. Change one detail and the answer changes — if the required fields had been specified and the evaluation showed their recall falling steadily after a product change, with nobody assigned to act on it, the gap would be iteration." }
  ],

  "7.1": [
    { h: "Three layers, three owners" },
    "Claude Code configuration sits in layers that match who owns a decision. The repository holds what the team agrees on: <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, <code>.claude/settings.json</code>, <code>.mcp.json</code>, and shared skills, agents and commands. Everyone who clones gets them, and changes go through review like code. The developer's own space holds preferences: <code>~/.claude/</code>, <code>CLAUDE.local.md</code> (ignored by git) and <code>.claude/settings.local.json</code>. Managed settings hold what the organisation requires. The classic failure — team standards placed in each developer's home directory — means a new joiner silently gets none of them.",
    { h: "Memory accumulates, settings override" },
    "The two kinds of file resolve differently, and scenarios turn on it. Every discovered <code>CLAUDE.md</code> is loaded and concatenated from broadest scope to most specific; none replaces another. Settings resolve by precedence, highest first: managed policy, command-line arguments, <code>.claude/settings.local.json</code>, <code>.claude/settings.json</code>, then <code>~/.claude/settings.json</code>. Arrays merge across layers and a scalar at a higher layer wins. Because managed policy cannot be overridden by a user or a project, it is the right place for anything that must hold across every repository.",
    "That gives the decision rule. A convention the team wants Claude to follow goes in project <code>CLAUDE.md</code> — it is instruction, and instruction is advisory. A rule the organisation must enforce goes in managed settings as a permission deny rule, with a PreToolUse hook where the check needs to inspect the actual command. Permission rules are evaluated deny, then ask, then allow, first match wins, so a deny is not undone by a broader allow elsewhere.",
    { h: "Guardrails, secrets and telemetry" },
    "Secrets never go in committed configuration. A shared <code>.mcp.json</code> references tokens through environment-variable expansion such as <code>${GITHUB_TOKEN}</code>, so the configuration is shared and the credential is not. In CI, run non-interactively with <code>-p</code> and a restricted tool set; bare mode (<code>--bare</code>) starts without hooks, skills, plugins, MCP servers, auto memory or <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, which is what makes every runner behave the same.",
    "Managed settings are also how telemetry is distributed. The enable flag only starts collection; nothing leaves the machine until an exporter and, for OTLP, an endpoint and protocol are set. A managed endpoint removes the conflicting per-developer variables, so a developer cannot redirect the organisation's telemetry elsewhere. Plugins and marketplaces distribute a whole working setup — skills, hooks, agents and MCP servers — as one unit; treat them as dependencies, pin them and review what their hooks may run.",
    { h: "Rolling it out" },
    "The sequence is pilot with one team, codify what worked in shared configuration, roll out with training, measure adoption and outcomes, iterate. Mandating first and codifying later produces exactly the inconsistency the configuration was meant to prevent.",
    { example: "A regulated firm requires that Claude Code never reads anything under a <code>secrets/</code> directory in any repository. A team lead has added \"Never read files in secrets/\" to one repository's <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, and some developers have broad read permissions in their user settings. Which change delivers the guarantee? Options: stronger wording in every repository's <code>CLAUDE.md</code>; a deny rule in each project's <code>.claude/settings.json</code>; a deny rule for that path in managed settings, backed by a hook; asking developers to remove the broad allow from their user settings. Managed settings are correct: they apply to every repository and cannot be overridden by user or project configuration, and deny rules are evaluated before allows. The project-level deny is the tempting answer and does work where it exists, but it covers only repositories that include it and can be edited by anyone with commit access. The <code>CLAUDE.md</code> line is instruction, not enforcement — memory files add to one another rather than override, and none of them block a tool call." }
  ],

  "7.2": [
    { h: "From individual wins to team capability" },
    "When two developers get large gains and sixty get none, the gains are tacit: prompts in their heads, personal skills in their home directories, habits nobody else has seen. The architect's job is to make that practice explicit and shared — skills, commands and <code>CLAUDE.md</code> conventions committed to each repository, with training on their use — so that the next developer inherits the workflow by cloning. Buying licences or banning the tools both miss the point; so does letting everyone configure their own, which guarantees inconsistent behaviour across teams.",
    { h: "Where the gains are, and the guard on each" },
    "Each high-value use comes with a practice that keeps it trustworthy. Code review assistance works best from an independent instance: the session that wrote a change retains its reasoning and is less likely to challenge its own decisions. In CI it runs non-interactively with <code>-p</code>, and <code>--output-format json</code> with <code>--json-schema</code> produces findings a pipeline can post as inline comments; passing prior review results into a re-review keeps it to new or unresolved issues. Test generation improves when testing standards and available fixtures are documented in <code>CLAUDE.md</code> and existing tests are in context. Migrations and large refactors use plan mode to investigate and agree the approach, then direct execution in small steps, each verified before the next.",
    "Accountability is the constraint over all of it. AI-generated changes go through the same review and test gates as any other change. \"The tests passed\" is not review: the tests check what someone thought to test, and if the same session wrote both the change and the tests, they share its blind spots.",
    { h: "Measuring it" },
    "Enthusiasm is not a metric, and neither is seat activation. Measure what the organisation cares about — cycle time, review turnaround, defect rates — before and after, so the roll-out can be judged and the practices that work can be codified further. If defect rates rise while cycle time falls, the gate is leaking.",
    { h: "Session hygiene" },
    "Claude Code sends the whole conversation with every request, so a session carried across unrelated tasks pays for stale context on every message and reasons over irrelevant history. Start fresh per task, compact within one long task when continuity matters — accepting that exact values can be lost in the summary — and begin each task with a clear definition of done. These are workflow habits worth writing into the team's conventions, not left to individuals.",
    { example: "An organisation adds an automated Claude Code review to CI. Developers ignore it: findings arrive as narrative paragraphs, and every push repeats the same comments. Leadership proposes letting pull requests auto-merge when the review raises nothing and the tests pass, to \"capture the productivity\". The better design keeps human review as the gate and fixes the review itself: structured output via <code>--output-format json</code> and <code>--json-schema</code> so each finding posts inline at its line, prior findings passed in so re-reviews report only new or unresolved issues, and review turnaround and escaped defects tracked. Auto-merge is the tempting answer because it removes friction, but it replaces accountability with the absence of a complaint from an automated reviewer the team already does not trust." }
  ],

  "7.3": [
    { h: "What a usable trace contains" },
    "You can only debug what you recorded. For an agent, a start and end timestamp tells on-call nothing; they need to reconstruct the run: the inputs, every tool call with its arguments, latency and outcome, the model's outputs, the prompt and model versions, the <code>request-id</code>, the <code>stop_reason</code>, and token counts including cache reads. With that record an incident at 02:00 can be replayed and explained. Without it, the only available responses are guesses — a bigger model, a longer context window — which the exam offers as distractors precisely because they change something without explaining anything.",
    { h: "Route by layer before you fix" },
    "The isolation method is simple: replay the exact request and look at the raw response. If the raw response is right and the application still misbehaves, the fault is in the integration layer. If the raw response is wrong, the fault is upstream — prompt, context, tools or model. If the response is faithful to the context but the context was wrong, the fault is in the data.",
    "Each layer has a different owner and fix. Integration failures announce themselves with an HTTP status: a 400 is a malformed request, such as a <code>tool_result</code> referencing an unknown <code>tool_use_id</code>, and an identical retry fails identically; a 429 is retried after honouring <code>retry-after</code>, except a spend-cap 429 that has none and needs an alert; 500 and 529 are retried with backoff. Model-output failures arrive inside a successful response — read <code>stop_reason</code> first, since <code>max_tokens</code> explains truncated JSON — and are fixed with structured outputs, prompts or evaluation. Data failures, such as a stale index or a withdrawn policy still retrievable, belong to whoever owns ingestion and refresh. Changing the model for a 4xx is the canonical mis-route.",
    { h: "Runbooks, rollback and AI in the loop" },
    "Runbooks cover the failures you can predict — rate limits, tool outages, retrieval staleness, model regressions — each with a detection signal, first steps, an escalation path and a rollback. Rollback of prompt and model versions only works if those are pinned, versioned artefacts (see 6.4). When AI tooling assists operations, start read-only: diagnostics, log analysis, a proposed fix. Anything that changes production sits behind human approval, because an incident is exactly when an agent's blast radius should be smallest.",
    "Every incident then feeds two things: an evaluation case, so the regression is caught before the next release, and a monitoring signal, so the next occurrence is detected by the system rather than by a customer.",
    { example: "A retail support agent starts quoting last season's 60-day return window instead of the new 30-day policy. The same morning, logs show sporadic 529 responses. The on-call engineer proposes moving to a larger model tier. The deciding detail is that the policy page was updated in the CMS last Tuesday and the nightly re-index job has been failing without alerting. These are two incidents in two layers. The 529s are transient integration errors, handled by retry with backoff, and are not why answers are wrong. The wrong policy is a data-layer fault: fix and re-run the index job, add a freshness alert on the index, and add an evaluation case for the new return window. The larger model is the tempting answer because the symptom looks like a quality problem, but a better model will faithfully repeat the stale policy it is given." }
  ]

};
